var tafsirData_15 = {
  "1": "\"Alif-Lãm-Ra\" opens this chapter with three disconnected letters, as several chapters do, and the position held by many early scholars is that their complete meaning rests with Allah. A frequently made observation is that these are ordinary letters of the Arabic alphabet, the same ones used in the poetry the Arabs prized, placed at the head of a Book they were invited to match and could not.\n\n\"These are the verses of the Book; the clear Quran.\" names what follows with two descriptions, and they work together.\n\nCalling it the Book indicates something written, fixed, and preserved rather than a collection of sayings. The definite form suggests the Book that had been expected, the one earlier scriptures pointed toward.\n\nThe word \"Quran\" comes from a root meaning to recite or read aloud, which describes how it was actually received and how it has been transmitted ever since. The Book is written and it is also recited, and these two aspects have preserved it in parallel for fourteen centuries: copies on paper and complete memorisation in the hearts of people in every generation.\n\n\"Clear\" translates an Arabic word indicating something that is itself plain and that also makes other things plain. Both senses apply. The Quran states its message without obscurity, and it clarifies matters human beings could never resolve on their own.\n\nThat description sets the tone for a chapter that will deal with people who found the message perfectly clear and rejected it anyway. Within a few verses they are quoted calling the Prophet insane and demanding that angels be sent down.\n\nThe opening therefore does something quiet but important. Before any of their objections are recorded, the reader is told what they were actually objecting to: a Book that is clear. Whatever their complaints amounted to, confusion was not among them.",
  "2": "\"˹The day will come when˺ the disbelievers will certainly wish they had submitted ˹to Allah˺.\" is the first statement in the chapter after its opening, and it looks forward to a moment of regret.\n\nThe Arabic construction indicates something that will happen repeatedly, often understood as a wish that recurs whenever they see the consequences of their choice. Commentators mentioned that this wish comes at various points: at death, in the grave, at the reckoning, and above all when believers are admitted to Paradise while they are not.\n\nThere is a report explained by some early scholars that when sinful believers are eventually removed from the Fire through Allah's mercy, those who remain wish most bitterly that they had been Muslims.\n\nThe word for what they will wish they had been indicates those who submit, who surrender themselves to Allah. This is the definition of the word Muslim, and the Quran applies it to the followers of every prophet.\n\nThe verse repays reflection because of what it says about desire. These were people who believed they were choosing freedom by refusing, and who treated submission as a loss of independence. The verse states that the same people will one day want nothing more than the thing they refused.\n\nThat reversal is worth noticing while there is still time to act on it. A person can examine his own resistance to a religious obligation and ask a simple question: will I regret this later? Most reluctance in matters of worship comes from valuing the present far above the future, which is exactly the miscalculation this verse identifies.\n\nPlacing this at the very start of the chapter is deliberate. Before any argument is presented, the outcome is announced, which frames everything that follows as a warning delivered while the wish can still be acted on.",
  "3": "\"˹So˺ let them eat and enjoy themselves and be diverted by ˹false˺ hope\" is not permission, and reading it as such would miss its force entirely.\n\nThe Arabic imperative here functions as a warning delivered with heavy irony, telling them to carry on with what they are doing since the outcome is already known. The same form appeared at the end of Surah Ibrahim, where the Prophet was told to say enjoy yourselves and that the destination is the Fire.\n\nThree activities are named, and together they describe a complete way of life.\n\nEating stands for physical appetite, the pursuit of what the body wants. Enjoyment covers pleasure more broadly. Neither is condemned in itself, and Islam does not treat food and enjoyment as evils.\n\nThe third item is the one that turns a normal life into a wasted one. The Arabic word \"amal\" indicates hope, expectation, the long chain of plans and assumptions a person carries about the future. Being diverted by it means letting those expectations occupy the mind so completely that nothing else registers.\n\nThis is the mechanism by which most people avoid thinking about death. Not by arguing against it, but by always having something ahead: a project to finish, a stage of life to reach, a plan that will make everything settle. The Prophet drew a picture of exactly this, described in a hadith recorded by al-Bukhari, drawing lines to show a man's hopes stretching beyond the point where his lifespan ends.\n\n\"For they will soon know.\" closes the verse, and the word for soon is doing quiet work.\n\nThe chapter had already placed a one-line preview of that knowledge in the verse before this one, saying the disbelievers will certainly wish they had submitted. The hoping that occupies them now and the wishing that awaits them are opposites: one pushes the reckoning out of mind, the other would trade everything to undo what was pushed aside.\n\nFrom a human standpoint, the reckoning seems distant. From the standpoint of the One speaking, it is imminent. Both are true, and the difference between them is exactly the gap that false hope fills.",
  "4": "\"We have never destroyed a society without a destined term.\" answers a question raised by the warning in the previous verses.\n\nIf people are being told they will soon know, why has nothing happened yet? The answer is that destruction, when it comes, arrives at an appointed time that was fixed in advance.\n\nThe Arabic phrase indicates a known, written decree. Nothing in the sequence is improvised. A community's period of grace is not extended because Allah is hesitating, and it does not end because His patience runs out.\n\nThe verse also makes a point about how divine punishment has always operated. It says we have never destroyed a society without such a term, which means every case recorded in the Quran followed this pattern. Noah's people were given generations. Thamud were given three days after killing the camel. Pharaoh was given plague after plague with opportunities to relent between each one.\n\nThere is something in this for understanding history more generally. Civilisations do not collapse the moment they become corrupt. There is usually a long period in which the decay is visible and the structure still stands, and people inside that period conclude that nothing is wrong precisely because nothing has happened yet.\n\nThe Prophet's audience in Mecca was living inside such a period, and they treated the delay as their argument. Surah Hud recorded them asking what was holding the punishment back.\n\nUnderstanding the appointed term changes how a believer reads both his own situation and the world around him. Injustice continuing does not mean it is permitted, and a warning unfulfilled does not mean it was empty.\n\nIt also removes the temptation to predict. Since the term is known only to Allah, nobody can announce when a community's time has come, and claims of that kind have always been the mark of people speaking without knowledge.",
  "5": "\"No people can advance their doom, nor can they delay it.\" completes the principle stated in the previous verse, and it closes both directions at once.\n\nThe Arabic wording covers the whole possibility. A community cannot bring its appointed end forward, and it cannot push it back. Whatever it does, the timing holds.\n\nThis exact formulation appears in several places in the Quran. Surah al-A'raf states it about every community having an appointed term, and Surah Yunus repeats it in the context of people demanding to know when the threatened punishment would arrive.\n\nThe repetition across chapters is worth noticing, because it addresses two different kinds of people with the same sentence.\n\nThe first are those who mock, demanding that the punishment be hurried along as proof. Surah Yunus recorded them doing exactly this, and asked whether the wicked realise what they are asking to be hastened. This verse tells them their demand changes nothing.\n\nThe second are those who assume they can postpone consequences indefinitely through wealth, alliances, or precautions. That is a more common attitude, and it operates quietly in prosperous societies. Preparations are made, defences are built, and the assumption grows that the arrangement can be sustained. The verse says the term does not move.\n\nThe Arabic word used here indicates the appointed time itself, and its use for both individuals and communities is deliberate. What applies to a nation applies to a person. Nobody dies early and nobody dies late, whatever the immediate cause appears to be.\n\nThere is a settling effect in believing this properly. Fear of death produces a great deal of anxiety, much of it directed at avoiding what cannot be avoided. Accepting that the moment is fixed does not remove the instinct to be careful, since taking precautions is commanded, but it removes the illusion that carefulness is what keeps a person alive.",
  "6": "\"They say, \"O you to whom the Reminder is revealed! You must be insane!\"\" records an accusation with a strange feature built into it.\n\nNotice how they address him. They call him the one to whom the Reminder is revealed, and then immediately declare him mad. The two halves of the sentence contradict each other, and commentators have pointed this out. Most understood the opening phrase as sarcasm, mocking his claim rather than conceding it.\n\nEither way the accusation itself was their standard weapon. The Arabic word indicates possession by jinn, being touched in the mind, which was the common explanation offered for anyone whose behaviour departed from expectation.\n\nThe charge was convenient rather than considered. If the man is unwell, his message requires no answer. Nobody debates a madman, and dismissing him costs nothing.\n\nWhat makes the accusation so weak is the character of the person it was aimed at. The Meccans had known him for forty years before he began preaching. They called him the trustworthy one, deposited their valuables with him, and asked him to settle their disputes. Nothing in his conduct, speech, or judgement suggested instability, and the Quran responds elsewhere by pointing out that their companion is not mad, and that by the favour of his Lord he is not possessed.\n\nThis accusation was not new to him. Surah adh-Dhariyat observes that no messenger came to those before them without their saying he was a magician or a madman, and asks whether they had all instructed one another to say it.\n\nThere is a pattern here that outlasts the seventh century. When a message cannot be answered, the messenger's competence becomes the topic. It is easier to question someone's sanity, motives, or stability than to engage with what he actually said, and the effect is the same: the conversation ends without anyone having to think.",
  "7": "\"\"Why do you not bring us the angels, if what you say is true?\"\" is the demand that follows their accusation, and it reveals what they thought would settle the matter.\n\nThey wanted visible angels. Not an argument, not a scripture, but beings descending in front of them, confirming the Prophet's claim beyond any possibility of doubt.\n\nThe demand appears repeatedly in the Quran. Surah al-Furqan records them asking why an angel was not sent down to him to be a warner alongside him, and Surah al-An'am notes that if an angel had been sent, the matter would have been decided and they would have been given no respite.\n\nThat last point is the crucial one, and the next verse states it directly. Angels arriving in visible form is not a neutral demonstration. In the accounts recorded throughout the Quran, when angels come openly to a rejecting people, they come to carry out the sentence. Lot's visitors arrived as guests and left as the instrument of a city's destruction.\n\nSo the request was more dangerous than the people making it understood. They were asking for the one event that would end the period of grace the chapter has just described.\n\nThere is also a deeper problem with the demand, and the following verses expose it. People who refuse a message do not usually refuse it for lack of evidence, and the chapter goes on to say that even if a gate to heaven were opened and they climbed through it, they would claim their eyes had been tricked.\n\nThe condition attached, \"if what you say is true,\" has the tone of a challenge rather than an inquiry. It is the same phrase the peoples of Noah and Hud used when daring their prophets to bring on the promised punishment.\n\nA person genuinely investigating a claim examines the evidence available. A person setting terms for what would convince him has usually decided already.",
  "8": "\"We do not send the angels down except for a just cause\" answers the demand of the previous verse, and it corrects the assumption behind it.\n\nThe Arabic indicates that angels descend only with the truth, meaning with a decisive matter: revelation delivered to a prophet, or the execution of a decree. They are not sent to satisfy curiosity or to perform for an audience that has set conditions.\n\nThis is worth understanding properly, because the demand for angels sounds reasonable on its surface. Why not simply provide the evidence people ask for?\n\nThe answer lies in what such an appearance would mean. Throughout the Quran, when angels appear openly to a community that has rejected its messenger, they arrive to carry out judgement. The angels who visited Abraham with good news continued on to Lot's people, and the city was overturned that morning.\n\n\"And then ˹the end of˺ the disbelievers will not be delayed.\" states this outcome plainly. Once that door opens, the appointed term the chapter described a few verses earlier expires immediately.\n\nSo the demand, if granted, would have destroyed the people making it. What they took for a fair test was in fact a request to have their period of grace cancelled.\n\nThere is a mercy in the refusal that they could not perceive. Many of those same Meccans later became believers. Umar ibn al-Khattab, Khalid ibn al-Walid, and Amr ibn al-As all opposed the Prophet fiercely before accepting Islam, and none of them would have had the opportunity if their earlier demand had been met.\n\nThis is a recurring feature of how the Quran presents unanswered requests. What people ask for is not always what would benefit them, and a refusal can be a form of protection. The delay they mocked was the space in which some of them eventually changed.",
  "9": "\"It is certainly We Who have revealed the Reminder, and it is certainly We Who will preserve it.\" is one of the most consequential statements in the Quran, and its two halves belong together.\n\nThe first half answers the accusations recorded a few verses earlier. They had called the Prophet insane and demanded angels. The reply identifies the source of the message: it was sent down by Allah, not produced by the man delivering it.\n\nThe Arabic word \"dhikr,\" rendered here as the Reminder, is used throughout the Quran as a name for the revelation itself. It indicates something that reminds, and the choice of name reflects what the Book does: it recalls to people what heedlessness had buried.\n\nThe second half is the promise. Allah undertakes to preserve it Himself.\n\nThis distinguishes the Quran from earlier scriptures in a way the Quran itself notes. Surah al-Ma'idah mentions that earlier communities were entrusted with the preservation of what was given to them, and describes some of them altering words from their places. Here the responsibility is not delegated.\n\nThe historical outcome is open to examination. The Quran was memorised in full by companions during the Prophet's lifetime, written down as it was revealed, compiled into a single volume under Abu Bakr, and standardised under Uthman. Since then it has been transmitted through two parallel channels: written copies and complete memorisation. Millions of people alive today have the entire text in memory, and a child in Nigeria recites the same words as a child in Indonesia.\n\nNo other book of comparable age has anything resembling this. That is not offered as proof to someone who rejects the claim, but it is a fact that anyone can verify.\n\nFor believers, the promise carries a responsibility rather than an excuse for passivity. Allah preserves it through people, and those who memorise, teach, and transmit it are the means by which the promise operates.",
  "10": "\"Indeed, We sent messengers before you ˹O Prophet˺ among the groups of early peoples\" turns from the promise of preservation to the consolation of history.\n\nThe Arabic word for these groups indicates factions, parties, the communities into which earlier humanity divided. Messengers were sent among them, which means the Prophet's situation was not unprecedented.\n\nThis form of consolation appears constantly in the Meccan chapters, and its method is worth noticing. He is not told that his difficulties will end soon, and he is given no timetable. He is shown that what he is experiencing is the standard experience of the role.\n\nThere is real relief in that for anyone in a difficult position. Much of the weight of hardship comes from the suspicion that something has gone wrong, that this was not supposed to happen. Discovering that the difficulty is normal for the work does not remove it, but it removes the additional burden of confusion.\n\nThe mention of early peoples also establishes continuity. The Prophet was not founding a new religion but continuing a line, and the chapters before this one had recounted that line in detail: Noah, Hud, Salih, Lot, Shu'ayb, Abraham, Joseph, and Moses.\n\nFor the Meccans, the same statement carried a different implication. They treated the Prophet's claim as an innovation, something strange appearing in their city. The verse tells them that what is happening among them has happened repeatedly, and the chapters preceding this one had already shown how those episodes ended.\n\nThe verse also prepares the ground for what follows. Having established that messengers were sent, the next verse states the reception they received, and it is the same reception the Prophet was getting in the streets of Mecca.\n\nPlaced together, the two verses form a single argument: this has all happened before, including the part that hurts.",
  "11": "\"But no messenger ever came to them without being mocked.\" completes the statement begun in the previous verse, and the word \"no\" is absolute.\n\nNot most messengers, not many of them, but every single one. There is no exception in the record, which means mockery is not a sign that something has gone wrong with a mission. It is part of the standard experience.\n\nThe Arabic verb indicates ridiculing someone, treating him as an object of amusement.\n\nThe chapters just before this one supplied the evidence in detail. Noah was laughed at while building his ship, and told his people that if they mocked him then he would mock them in turn. Hud's people said one of their gods had struck him with madness. Salih's people expressed disappointment that a promising man had gone astray. Shu'ayb was sarcastically called tolerant and sensible. Moses was called a magician.\n\nThe Prophet Muhammad received the same treatment in the same words. He had just been called insane a few verses earlier in this chapter.\n\nThere is a reason mockery is so consistently the weapon of choice, and it is worth naming. Ridicule requires no argument, no evidence, and no risk. It ends a conversation without anyone having to think, and it allows an audience to feel superior while remaining ignorant. Against a message that cannot be refuted, it is the most efficient available response.\n\nUnderstanding this changes how a believer receives it. Being laughed at for practising one's faith is not evidence of being wrong, and it is not a sign of failure in how the matter was presented.\n\nThere is also a warning implied for anyone who finds mockery easy. The Quran records this behaviour as characteristic of the people who were eventually destroyed, and Surah al-An'am notes that those who ridiculed were surrounded by the very thing they had ridiculed.",
  "12": "\"This is how We allow disbelief ˹to steep˺ into the hearts of the wicked.\" describes what happens inside people who respond to messengers with mockery.\n\nThe Arabic indicates inserting something into the heart, threading it through so that it passes into the interior. Commentators explained it as disbelief entering and settling in a heart that has made itself receptive to it.\n\nThe verse needs careful handling, because it can be misread as saying that Allah forces people into disbelief and then punishes them for it. The Quran rules that reading out repeatedly. Surah Yunus stated plainly that Allah does not wrong people in the least but that people wrong themselves, and the chapters before this described hearts being sealed only after persistent, knowing refusal.\n\nThe opening phrase, \"this is how,\" points backward to the previous verse. It follows the mockery. Their ridicule of messenger after messenger produced a condition, and the condition was then confirmed.\n\nThere is a pattern in this that anyone can observe in ordinary life. A person who repeatedly dismisses something eventually becomes incapable of taking it seriously. The first refusal requires an effort of will; the hundredth requires nothing at all, because the response has become automatic.\n\nThe description of those affected as wicked is significant. The Arabic term indicates criminals, people who cut themselves off from what is right through deliberate action. The verse is not describing the honestly confused or those who never received a clear message.\n\nWhat this means practically is that mockery is more dangerous to the mocker than to his target. It feels like a position of strength, and it is a process by which a heart closes.\n\nThe following verses show the result: people who would not believe despite the examples of destroyed nations, and who would explain away a gate opened in the sky.",
  "13": "\"They would not believe in this ˹Quran˺ despite the ˹many˺ examples of those ˹destroyed˺ before.\" states the outcome of the condition described in the previous verse.\n\nThe Arabic indicates that the way of earlier peoples has already passed, meaning the precedent is established and known. What happened to those communities is not a secret or a matter of speculation.\n\nThe Meccans had access to this information in several forms. The Quran had recounted the accounts of Noah, Ad, Thamud, Lot, and Midian at length in the chapters immediately preceding this one. They also had physical evidence, since their trade routes passed the ruins of Thamud in the north and the region associated with Lot's cities.\n\nSo the refusal described here is not a refusal made in ignorance. It happens with the examples fully available, which is what makes it so striking.\n\nThere is something worth reflecting on about why examples fail to persuade. History provides the clearest evidence available about consequences, and it is almost universally ignored. Every generation watches the previous one make mistakes and repeats them, often with complete confidence that its own situation is different.\n\nThe reason is usually not a lack of information but a sense of exemption. People accept that a pattern exists and assume they stand outside it. The Meccans knew what happened to Thamud and treated it as ancient history involving other people.\n\nSurah Yusuf made a similar observation, noting how many signs in the heavens and the earth people pass by with indifference.\n\nWhat follows in the next two verses pushes this to its limit. If examples from history do not work, what about direct experience? The chapter answers that question with an image so extreme that it settles the matter entirely, and it explains why the demand for angels a few verses earlier was never a genuine request for evidence.",
  "14": "\"And even if We opened for them a gate to heaven, through which they continued to ascend,\" begins a scenario designed to answer the demand made earlier in this chapter.\n\nThey had asked why angels were not sent down to them. The verse now imagines something considerably greater. Not angels descending, but a gate opened in the sky and the people themselves climbing through it.\n\nThe Arabic indicates continuous ascent, going up steadily. This is not a glimpse or a brief vision. They keep climbing, with the opening above them and the earth receding below.\n\nConsider what such an experience would involve. It would be sustained, physical, and shared by everyone present. There would be no question of a trick of the light or a moment of confusion, since the ascent continues and can be examined at leisure by many people at once.\n\nBy any reasonable standard, this is a demonstration beyond anything they had requested. It exceeds their own terms.\n\nThe verse is constructed this way deliberately, because it is building toward a conclusion about what evidence can and cannot achieve. The Quran has already stated in Surah al-An'am that even if angels had been sent down to them and the dead had spoken to them, they still would not have believed unless Allah willed.\n\nWhat makes this particular image so effective is that it removes every possible objection in advance. There is no ambiguity in it, no room for interpretation, and no dependence on trusting a messenger's report.\n\nThe scenario also exposes an assumption hidden inside their demand. They spoke as though belief were a passive event that evidence simply causes, the way heat melts wax. The Quran teaches otherwise. The heart is a participant, not a spectator, and what a person loves, fears, and protects shapes what he is able to see; evidence enters a heart that is already occupied.\n\nTheir response, given in the next verse, is therefore not an argument about evidence at all. It is a demonstration of something else entirely, and it explains why the chapter said a few verses earlier that disbelief had been threaded into their hearts.",
  "15": "\"\"Our eyes have truly been dazzled! In fact, we must have been bewitched.\" is the response the previous verse was building toward, and it is devastating.\n\nHaving ascended through an opened gate in the sky, they would conclude that their vision had been affected. The Arabic indicates eyes being sealed, blocked, or intoxicated so that they no longer report reality accurately.\n\nTheir second explanation goes further. They would decide they had been placed under a spell. This is the same accusation levelled at Moses in Pharaoh's court and at the Prophet in Mecca, now applied to their own senses.\n\nWhat the verse exposes is the nature of their refusal. It was never about insufficient evidence. A person who explains away a sustained physical experience shared by a crowd will explain away anything, because the explaining is the point.\n\nThis is worth stating carefully, because it is easy to turn into an accusation against anyone who asks questions. The Quran repeatedly invites people to look, examine, travel, and reason. Honest questions are welcomed throughout these chapters. What is being described here is different: a settled determination to reject, dressed in the language of scepticism.\n\nThere is a recognisable form of this in ordinary experience. When someone has decided what he wants to believe, contrary evidence does not change his position; it changes his theory about the evidence. The data was flawed, the witness was unreliable, the demonstration was staged.\n\nNotice also what their explanation costs them. To maintain their position they must abandon trust in their own eyes, which is a serious price. A person who declares his senses unreliable has undermined every other conclusion he has ever reached, including his confidence in his own judgement about the Prophet.\n\nThis is where refusal eventually leads, and the chapter has already explained how a heart arrives there.",
  "16": "\"Indeed, We have placed constellations in the sky, and adorned it for all to see.\" turns from the people who would explain away an opened heaven to the heaven itself, which stands above them every night without being explained away.\n\nThe Arabic word indicates great structures or stations in the sky, and commentators explained it as the major star groupings and the paths through which the sun and moon move across the year. The Arabs navigated by these, timed their travel by them, and knew them by name.\n\nTwo things are being said about the sky at once, and both matter.\n\nThe first is order. Constellations are fixed arrangements that return predictably, which is what makes navigation possible. A traveller crossing a featureless desert at night could find his direction from them, and Surah an-Nahl notes that people are guided by the stars.\n\nThe second is beauty. The sky is described as adorned, made attractive for those who look at it. This is not a functional statement but an aesthetic one, and the Quran mentions it deliberately.\n\nThat combination is worth pausing over. The sky did not have to be beautiful in order to work. Stars could have provided navigation while being ugly or unremarkable. The fact that the arrangement is both useful and beautiful says something about the One who arranged it, and the Quran often draws attention to this pairing in creation.\n\nThere is also a quiet reproach in the verse's placement. The people in the preceding verses demanded a sign in the sky and said they would dismiss it as a trick. Meanwhile a sign has been above them their entire lives, visible every clear night, and they had stopped looking at it.\n\nSurah Yusuf made this exact point, noting how many signs in the heavens and the earth people pass by with indifference. Familiarity, not lack of evidence, is the obstacle.",
  "17": "\"And We protected it from every accursed devil,\" adds a second fact about the sky, and it moves from what can be seen to what cannot.\n\nThe Arabic word for the protection indicates guarding something, keeping it safe from intrusion. The sky is described as secured.\n\nThose excluded are described with a word indicating one who is driven away, stoned, expelled from mercy. It is the same description attached to Satan in the phrase Muslims use when seeking refuge from him before recitation.\n\nThis verse and the one that follows address something the Meccans took seriously. Soothsayers in Arabia claimed access to hidden knowledge, and their claims carried real authority in a society without other means of predicting events. Some of them attributed their information to jinn who brought them news from above.\n\nThe Quran does not simply dismiss this world as imagination. It describes the arrangement accurately and explains its limits, which is a more useful correction than denial would be.\n\nThe relevance to the chapter's argument is direct. The Prophet had been accused of being insane and, elsewhere, of receiving his message the way soothsayers received theirs. This passage draws a clear line between revelation, which comes down protected and complete, and the fragments that were claimed by those who traded in hidden knowledge.\n\nSurah ash-Shu'ara addresses this explicitly, stating that the devils did not bring the Quran down, that it is not fitting for them, and that they are not able to do so.\n\nThe protection described here therefore serves the promise made a few verses earlier in this chapter. Allah said He would preserve the Reminder, and part of that preservation concerns the channel through which it descends.\n\nWhat happens to any devil who attempts to listen is described in the next verse, using an image the Arabs could observe with their own eyes.",
  "18": "\"Except the one eavesdropping, who is then pursued by a visible flare.\" completes the description of the guarded sky, and it gives the exception to the protection just mentioned.\n\nThe Arabic indicates one who steals a hearing, snatching a fragment by listening in. This is not access; it is theft attempted at the edge, and it is immediately answered.\n\nThe pursuit comes as a clear, piercing flame. The Arabic describes a burning brand or flare that is unmistakable, which connects the passage to something the Arabs observed regularly: the streaks of light that cross the night sky.\n\nThe Prophet explained this matter in a hadith recorded by al-Bukhari. He described how the angels speak of a matter decreed in the heavens, and how eavesdroppers snatch a word and pass it down, sometimes being struck before they can deliver it and sometimes conveying it. The fragment then reaches a soothsayer, who mixes it with a hundred lies, and when part of it turns out to be true, people say that he told them a thing that came to pass.\n\nThat explanation clarifies a great deal about how such claims worked. Occasional accuracy in a stream of invention is enough to sustain a reputation, and people remember the one prediction that landed while forgetting the hundred that did not.\n\nIt also draws the distinction the chapter needs. Revelation comes down complete and protected, as the earlier verse promised. What the soothsayers had was a stolen fragment wrapped in fabrication, and the difference between the two is the difference between a scripture and a rumour.\n\nThere is a practical application that has not aged. Claims to hidden knowledge remain a profitable business, and the method has not changed: a few accurate details, a great deal of vagueness, and an audience that remembers the hits. Islam closes this door firmly, and the Prophet warned that whoever visits a fortune-teller and believes him has rejected what was revealed.",
  "19": "\"As for the earth, We spread it out\" brings the argument down from the guarded sky to the ground people walk on.\n\nThe Arabic indicates extending something, stretching it out so that it becomes broad and usable. This describes the earth as it functions for those living on it, which is exactly what a person experiences: a surface laid out for habitation. A very large sphere is, for its inhabitants, spread out, and the Quran uses similar expressions elsewhere describing the earth as a resting place and a bed.\n\n\"And placed upon it firm mountains\" names the next feature. The Arabic word indicates immovable, anchoring masses. Modern geology describes mountains as having deep roots extending far below the surface, and the Quran elsewhere calls them pegs.\n\n\"And caused everything to grow there in perfect balance.\" completes the verse with a phrase worth examining closely.\n\nThe Arabic indicates growing something of every weighed thing, measured out precisely. Two ideas are contained in it. Everything that grows does so in determined quantities, and everything that grows does so in a proportion that fits.\n\nThat proportion is one of the most striking features of the natural world. The plants that grow produce exactly what animals need to breathe, and the animals produce what plants need in return. Soil composition, rainfall, temperature, and growing seasons combine in arrangements that support life rather than destroying it.\n\nThe word for weighing is significant in a Book that repeatedly connects the balance in creation with the balance humans are commanded to observe. Surah ar-Rahman places the raising of the sky and the setting of the balance side by side, and immediately instructs people not to transgress in the balance.\n\nThere is an argument running through these three verses that becomes clear when they are read together. The sky is ordered and protected; the earth is spread out and measured. Nothing about the arrangement suggests accident, and the people demanding an extraordinary sign were standing on one.",
  "20": "\"And We made in it means of sustenance for you\" continues the description of the earth, and it moves from how the ground is arranged to what it produces.\n\nThe Arabic word indicates the means of living, everything a person needs to sustain himself. This includes food and water, and it also covers the wider provision that makes settled life possible: materials, fuel, and the resources that human work depends on.\n\nPlacing this immediately after the mention of balanced growth is deliberate. The earth is not merely stable and orderly; it is stocked. Someone arriving on it finds what he needs already present.\n\n\"And others, who you do not provide for.\" is the phrase that transforms the verse, and it deserves careful reading.\n\nCommentators have understood these others in several complementary ways. Some took it to mean animals, birds, and livestock, whose sustenance grows on the same earth without any human arrangement. Others included servants, dependants, and family members whom a person imagines he supports but whose provision was actually written before he was involved. Some included every creature in existence beyond human awareness.\n\nAll of these readings carry the same point, and it is a humbling one. A person naturally sees himself as the provider for those under his care. He works, earns, and distributes, and it feels like a chain running from his effort to their food.\n\nThe verse quietly cuts that chain. He is not their provider. He is a means through which provision reaches them, and the provision was arranged by someone else.\n\nThis connects to what Surah Hud said about every moving creature on earth having its sustenance guaranteed by Allah. The insects in the soil, the birds overhead, and the creatures in the depths of the sea are all fed on the same earth, and nobody organises it.\n\nFor a person anxious about supporting a household, the reframing is worth absorbing.",
  "21": "\"There is not any means ˹of sustenance˺ whose reserves We do not hold\" makes a claim about everything that exists, and the image behind it is one of storehouses.\n\nThe Arabic word indicates treasuries, places where things are kept in store. Every kind of thing has its reserves with Allah, held and controlled.\n\nThis applies to rain, to crops, to minerals, to knowledge, to health, and to everything else a person might need. The verse says the source of each is not the visible mechanism but a reserve held elsewhere.\n\nThere is something clarifying in this for how a believer views the causes he sees around him. Rain comes from clouds, and clouds come from evaporation, and evaporation comes from heat. All of that is true and worth understanding. What the verse adds is that the whole chain is a means of distribution rather than the origin of the supply.\n\n\"Only bringing it forth in precise measure.\" states how the reserves are released.\n\nThe Arabic indicates a known, determined measure. Nothing is sent down randomly or in unlimited quantity.\n\nThat measurement is one of the conditions of life. Rain in the right amount produces crops, and the same rain multiplied becomes a flood that destroys them. Sunlight in measure allows growth, and beyond it burns. The Quran mentions elsewhere that Allah sends down water in due measure, and that if He expanded provision for His servants without limit they would transgress on the earth.\n\nThat last point is worth dwelling on. Restriction is often experienced as deprivation, and the Quran presents it as part of the arrangement rather than a failure of generosity. A person given everything he wanted immediately would be destroyed by it, and most people can verify this from their own history.\n\nWhat this verse offers, read alongside the ones before it, is a settled way of understanding provision: the reserves are full, the release is measured, and neither is in human hands.",
  "22": "\"We send fertilizing winds\" names something the Arabs knew from experience without knowing its mechanism.\n\nThe Arabic word indicates winds that impregnate or fertilise. Commentators explained this as winds that carry water vapour and drive clouds together until rain falls, and they also noted the role of wind in carrying pollen from plant to plant.\n\nBoth processes are real, and both were understood only in outline by anyone at the time. Wind carries pollen between male and female parts of plants, which is essential for fruit to form. Wind also drives moisture-laden air and pushes cloud masses together, which is part of how rain is produced. The single Arabic word covers both, and the choice is remarkably apt for a description given in seventh-century Arabia.\n\n\"And bring down rain from the sky for you to drink.\" states the result, and it selects drinking as the purpose to mention.\n\nRain has many uses: agriculture, livestock, washing, and the replenishment of wells. The verse names the most basic and the most immediate. A person can live without crops for a while; he cannot live long without water.\n\nSurah al-Waqi'ah presses this point with a question, asking who sends the water down from the clouds, and noting that if Allah willed He could make it bitter, so why are people not grateful.\n\n\"It is not you who hold its reserves.\" closes the verse by connecting it back to the storehouses mentioned a moment earlier.\n\nThe reserves of water are not in human hands. This remains true despite dams, reservoirs, and desalination plants. Every one of those depends on water that arrived from somewhere, and no human arrangement produces a single drop.\n\nThere is a quiet correction in this for a species that has become confident in its ability to manage resources. Managing a supply and creating one are entirely different activities, and the verse points out which of the two people actually do.",
  "23": "\"Surely it is We Who give life and cause death.\" moves the argument from provision to the two events that frame every existence.\n\nThe pairing is deliberate and the order matters. Life comes first, then death, following the sequence a person actually experiences.\n\nBoth are attributed directly to Allah, and this is worth pausing over in each case. Nobody understands how life begins. Biology can describe the processes surrounding it in increasing detail, and the moment at which inert material becomes a living thing remains outside human reach. Nobody can produce it, and nobody can prevent its departure when the time comes.\n\nAttributing death to Allah changes how a believer holds it. Death is not an accident, a system failure, or the final victory of an illness. It arrives at the appointed term this chapter described earlier, which can be neither advanced nor delayed.\n\nThere is also an argument for resurrection built into the statement, and the Quran makes it repeatedly. The One who produced life from nothing is not going to find its restoration difficult.\n\n\"And We are the ˹Eternal˺ Successor.\" closes the verse with a name that carries a striking image.\n\nThe Arabic indicates the one who remains after others are gone, the inheritor. When a person dies, his property passes to those who survive him. The verse extends this to its final conclusion: when everyone has passed, everything returns to the One who remains.\n\nThis is a sobering way to think about ownership. Nobody permanently owns anything. Every possession a person holds is currently between owners, and the sequence of inheritance has a final destination.\n\nSurah Maryam states the same, that Allah will inherit the earth and everyone on it. Surah Ali Imran instructs believers to remember that to Allah belongs the inheritance of the heavens and the earth.\n\nFor a chapter addressing people absorbed in eating, enjoyment, and long hopes, the reminder is precisely placed.",
  "24": "\"We certainly know those who have gone before you and those who will come after ˹you˺.\" makes a statement about knowledge that spans the whole of human history in both directions.\n\nThe Arabic wording has been understood in more than one way by commentators, and the range is worth noting honestly. Many read it as referring to earlier and later generations of humanity, which is the reading reflected here. Others explained it as those who come forward in the ranks of prayer and those who come later, or those who go forward in doing good and those who lag behind. Some applied it to the ranks of armies.\n\nAll these readings share a single meaning: nobody is unknown, whatever his position in time or in a line.\n\nRead as generations, the verse addresses something a person rarely thinks about. Billions have lived and died, most of them leaving no record at all. Their names are gone, their circumstances forgotten, and no archive holds any trace of them. Billions more are yet to be born.\n\nEvery one of them is known individually. Not as a statistic or a category, but as a person with a life, intentions, and deeds.\n\nThere is comfort in this that is easy to overlook. Human beings fear insignificance, and the fear is reasonable given the numbers involved. A person who lives quietly, harms nobody, does good that is never recorded, and dies without notice has vanished as far as history is concerned.\n\nThe verse says otherwise. Being unrecorded by people is not the same as being unknown, and Surah ar-Ra'd stated that not an atom's weight escapes His knowledge.\n\nThere is also a warning in it for those who assume anonymity protects them. Wrongdoing committed where nobody was watching, in a generation long past or one still to come, is equally present to the One described here.\n\nThe verse that follows draws the practical conclusion: all of them will be gathered.",
  "25": "\"Surely your Lord ˹alone˺ will gather them together ˹for judgment˺.\" follows naturally from the statement that all generations are known.\n\nKnowledge and gathering belong together. A record without an accounting would be pointless, and an accounting without complete knowledge would be unjust. The previous verse established the first, and this one supplies the second.\n\nThe Arabic verb indicates collecting and assembling, bringing scattered things into one place. Everyone who has ever lived, from the first human being to the last, in a single gathering.\n\nThat claim raises the obvious objection, which the Quran addresses elsewhere. Bodies have decomposed, been burned, drowned, or scattered across continents over thousands of years. Surah Yasin records a man holding up a decayed bone and asking who will give it life, and the answer given is that the One who produced it the first time will revive it.\n\nThis chapter has already supplied the same argument in a different form, stating a few verses earlier that it is Allah who gives life and causes death.\n\nThe word \"your Lord\" is worth noticing in a verse addressed to the Prophet during a period of intense opposition. The gathering is described as something his Lord will do, which carries assurance rather than only warning. The people mocking him would be present at it.\n\n\"He is truly All-Wise, All-Knowing.\" closes with two names that answer the two questions such a gathering raises.\n\nKnowledge answers whether anything will be missed. Nothing will, since the previous verse established that every generation is known.\n\nWisdom answers whether the outcome will be right. The Arabic indicates placing everything exactly where it belongs, which is what distinguishes a judgement from a mere exercise of power.\n\nTogether they describe a reckoning that is both complete and correct, which is more than any human court has ever managed.",
  "26": "\"Indeed, We created man from sounding clay moulded from black mud.\" begins the account of human origins that occupies the next several verses, and the description of the material is unusually specific.\n\nTwo terms are used. The first indicates dried clay that makes a sound when struck, the kind of hardened earth that rings hollow. The second indicates dark mud that has been altered by standing, aged and changed in smell and texture.\n\nThe Quran describes the material of human creation in several places using different terms, and commentators have generally understood these as stages rather than competing accounts. Surah as-Sajdah mentions the beginning of human creation from clay, Surah ar-Rahman describes it as clay like pottery, and Surah al-Hijr here specifies the aged, darkened mud that was dried into sounding clay.\n\nWhat is worth noticing is the ordinariness of the material. Not gold, not light, not anything precious. Aged mud, dried until it rings.\n\nThis has a purpose in the passage that follows immediately. Within a few verses, Iblis refuses to prostrate and gives exactly this as his reason: he will not bow to a human made from sounding clay moulded from black mud. The material is mentioned in advance so that the reader understands what the objection was based on.\n\nThere is something the Quran establishes here about human dignity, and it works by contrast. Whatever honour human beings receive does not come from what they are made of. It comes from what was breathed into them and from the command given to the angels concerning them, both described in the verses ahead.\n\nThat distinction matters. A person tempted to measure worth by material, ancestry, or origin is looking at exactly the thing Iblis looked at. The Prophet stated in his farewell sermon that no Arab has superiority over a non-Arab except through piety, and that all are from Adam, and Adam was from dust.",
  "27": "\"As for the jinn, We created them earlier from smokeless fire.\" introduces the other created beings who share the responsibility of belief and accountability.\n\nTwo facts are given, and both matter for what follows.\n\nThe first is timing. The jinn were created before humanity. This becomes relevant immediately, since Iblis was of the jinn, as Surah al-Kahf states explicitly, and the passage that follows describes him refusing to bow to a creature he considered inferior and newer.\n\nThe second is material. The Arabic indicates a scorching fire, and commentators explained it as fire without smoke, intense and penetrating. Where human beings come from aged mud, the jinn come from flame.\n\nThe Quran treats the jinn as real beings rather than symbols. They are described as having free will, being addressed by revelation, and being accountable, and Surah al-Jinn records a group of them listening to the Quran and believing. Surah adh-Dhariyat states that jinn and humans were created only to worship Allah, placing both under the same purpose.\n\nIt is worth being clear about what Islam does not teach here. The jinn are not ghosts of the dead, and they are not gods or intermediaries. Pre-Islamic Arabs sought protection from them and consulted those who claimed contact with them, and the Quran corrects this, mentioning men who used to seek refuge with males of the jinn and only increased them in burden.\n\nThe contrast of materials in these two verses sets up everything that follows. Fire is quick, rising, and dramatic. Clay is slow, low, and unremarkable.\n\nThe refusal recorded a few verses later rests entirely on that comparison, and the passage exists partly to show what happens when a creature ranks itself by material rather than by obedience.\n\nThe pride that follows in this chapter grows straight out of this difference of materials. When Iblis explains his refusal, his argument is precisely a hierarchy of substances: fire, as he sees it, outranks mud. The verse quietly undercuts him in advance, because the worth of a creature is settled by the One who made it, not by what He made it from.",
  "28": "\"˹Remember, O Prophet˺ when your Lord said to the angels, \"I am going to create a human being from sounding clay moulded from black mud.\"\" moves from the description of materials to the moment of announcement.\n\nThe angels are informed in advance. This is significant in itself, since Allah owes no explanation to His creation, and the announcement indicates that something of importance was being introduced into the order of things.\n\nThe material is repeated word for word from the earlier verse. This repetition is deliberate, and it does something important for the passage ahead. The angels were told exactly what this creature would be made from, and they submitted anyway. Iblis was given the same information and used it as his objection.\n\nThe same fact produced obedience in one group and refusal in another, which locates the problem in the responder rather than in the information.\n\nSurah al-Baqarah records more of this exchange, where Allah tells the angels He is placing a successor on the earth, and they ask about one who will cause corruption and shed blood, and are told that He knows what they do not. Their question was an inquiry, not an objection, and they accepted the answer immediately.\n\nThat distinction is worth holding on to. Asking a question about what one does not understand is not the same as refusing what one has been told, and the Quran presents the angels' inquiry without any criticism.\n\nThe description of Allah as \"your Lord,\" addressed to the Prophet, keeps the account connected to the situation in Mecca. This is not a story told for its own sake. The Prophet was facing people who considered themselves too important to follow a man they judged by his lack of wealth and status, and the passage about to unfold concerns exactly that form of self-assessment.\n\nWhat follows is the command that turned an announcement into a test.",
  "29": "\"\"So when I have fashioned him and had a spirit of My Own ˹creation˺ breathed into him, fall down in prostration to him.\"\" completes the announcement with a command, and it contains the moment that gives human beings whatever dignity they possess.\n\nTwo stages are described. First the fashioning, indicating that the form is completed and proportioned. Then the breathing of the spirit, which is what turns a shaped body into a living, conscious being.\n\nThe Arabic phrase indicating a spirit of His own creation has been discussed carefully by scholars, and precision here matters. It does not mean that a part of Allah entered the human being, which would be impossible. The attribution to Himself is an attribution of honour, in the way that the Kaaba is called the House of Allah without any suggestion that He dwells in it. The spirit is created; the connection to Him is a mark of dignity.\n\nWhat that spirit actually is remains outside human knowledge. Surah al-Isra addresses this directly, saying that when people ask about the spirit, the answer is that it is from the command of the Lord, and that they have been given only a little knowledge.\n\n\"Fall down in prostration to him.\" is the command given to the angels, and it requires clarification.\n\nThis was a prostration of honour and greeting, not of worship. Worship belongs to Allah alone, and the command came from Him. A similar prostration of respect appears in the story of Joseph, where his parents and brothers bowed before him, and the Prophet Muhammad later closed that door completely, refusing to allow prostration to any created being.\n\nThere is something worth noticing about the sequence. The command comes after the spirit is breathed in, which means the angels were not being told to honour clay. They were told to honour what Allah had placed within it.",
  "30": "\"So the angels prostrated all together—\" reports the response, and its brevity is part of its meaning.\n\nNo discussion is recorded. No hesitation, no request for clarification, no interval between the command and the compliance. The verse moves directly from the instruction to the act.\n\nThe Arabic emphasises completeness twice over, indicating all of them, together, without exception. Every angel, at the same moment.\n\nThis is how the Quran consistently describes angels. Surah at-Tahrim says they do not disobey Allah in what He commands them and do what they are ordered. Surah al-Anbiya describes them as not preceding Him in speech and acting by His command.\n\nThat obedience is not the result of a struggle. Angels are not described as resisting temptation and overcoming it, since they were not created with conflicting desires. Their nature and their obedience are the same thing.\n\nUnderstanding this makes the human situation clearer rather than diminishing it. A being who obeys without any pull in the opposite direction is doing something different from a person who obeys while wanting otherwise. The angels' compliance is perfect; the human struggle to comply is what human worship consists of.\n\nThere is also something instructive in what the angels had just been told. They knew this creature was made from aged mud dried into sounding clay. They had earlier asked about one who would cause corruption and shed blood, and had been told that Allah knows what they do not.\n\nHaving received that answer, they did not raise it again.\n\nOne detail is worth settling, since the scene is often misread. The prostration was an act of obedience directed to Allah, with Adam as its direction, in the way Muslims pray toward the Kaaba without worshipping the building. Honouring what Allah has commanded to be honoured is itself service to Allah, and the angels understood the command exactly that way.\n\nThe contrast with what comes next is the entire point of the passage. The same command, delivered in the same moment, to beings standing in the same place. All of them complied except one, and the exception is named immediately.",
  "31": "\"But not Iblîs, who refused to prostrate with the others.\" introduces the exception, and the wording identifies precisely what he did.\n\nThe Arabic indicates that he declined and refused to be among those prostrating. Two verbs are used, and together they describe a decision rather than a hesitation. He did not fail to prostrate through weakness or confusion. He refused.\n\nIblis is named here as a specific individual. The Quran states in Surah al-Kahf that he was of the jinn and departed from the command of his Lord, which explains how a being present among the angels could disobey when angels do not. He was created from fire, as this chapter mentioned a few verses earlier, and he was in their company without sharing their nature.\n\nThe phrase about not being with those who prostrated is worth noticing. He separated himself from a group, and the separation was public. Every other being present complied, and one stood apart.\n\nThere is something in this about the nature of pride, and the passage develops it in the verses ahead. Iblis had knowledge that many creatures lack. He knew Allah, knew the command came from Him, and never denied His existence for a moment. His problem was not disbelief in the ordinary sense.\n\nThat is worth taking seriously, because it separates two things people often merge. Knowing the truth and submitting to it are different acts. A person can be entirely convinced and still refuse, and the refusal comes from somewhere other than the intellect.\n\nThe Prophet defined arrogance in a hadith recorded by Muslim as rejecting the truth and looking down on people. Both halves of that definition appear in this account: a truth rejected, and a creature looked down upon.\n\nThe exchange that follows shows Allah questioning him directly, which is itself remarkable, and gives him the opportunity to explain himself.",
  "32": "\"Allah asked, \"O Iblîs! What is the matter with you that you did not join others in prostration?\"\" opens a direct exchange, and the fact that a question is asked at all deserves attention.\n\nAllah does not need information. The question is not an inquiry but an opportunity, giving the offender the chance to state his position and hear it stated.\n\nThis pattern appears elsewhere in the Quran. Adam and his wife were asked whether they had not been forbidden that tree. Jesus will be asked whether he told people to take him and his mother as gods. In each case the question exposes something rather than discovering it.\n\nThere is a principle of justice in this that Islamic law later developed. An accused person is heard before judgement is passed, even when the facts are established. Being made to account for oneself is part of what makes a verdict just rather than merely correct.\n\nThe form of address is worth noticing. He is called by name, which makes the confrontation personal. This is not a general announcement about disobedience; it is one individual being singled out in front of everyone who had complied.\n\nThe question also identifies the offence precisely: not joining those who prostrated. The emphasis falls on separation from the group as much as on the act itself. Every other being present had done it, which removes any suggestion that the command was unclear or impossible.\n\nWhat makes this exchange so significant for the chapter's argument is where it leads. Iblis is about to give his reason, and his reason will turn out to be a comparison of materials. He is not going to claim he misunderstood, was unable, or did not hear.\n\nHe will explain that he considered himself too good for it, which is the position the Meccan leaders held toward a message delivered by a man they judged beneath them.",
  "33": "\"\"It is not for me to prostrate to a human You created from sounding clay moulded from black mud.\" is the answer, and it is worth examining word by word, because everything wrong with it is on the surface.\n\nThe opening phrase indicates that such an act would not be fitting for someone like him. He is not saying he cannot; he is saying it is beneath him.\n\nHis reasoning is a comparison of materials. He had been created from fire, as this chapter stated earlier, and the object of the command was made from aged mud dried into clay. Surah al-A'raf records him making the comparison explicitly, saying he is better because he was created from fire and this one from clay.\n\nThe argument fails on several levels at once, and each failure is instructive.\n\nFirst, it answers a command with an opinion. He was not asked to evaluate the creature; he was told to prostrate. Obedience does not become conditional on agreeing with the instruction, and treating it that way is the essence of what happened here.\n\nSecond, his ranking of materials is his own. Nobody told him fire outranks clay. He assumed it, and the assumption seemed obvious to him because fire is dramatic and clay is not. Clay, however, is stable, holds shape, receives water, and grows things. Fire consumes and leaves ash.\n\nThird, he was looking at the wrong thing entirely. The command came after the spirit was breathed in, so the honour being recognised was never about the material.\n\nHis response also contains a subtle insolence in the way he describes the creature. He mentions the mud rather than the human being, reducing the object of the command to its least impressive component.\n\nThis is how contempt operates. It selects one true detail and lets it stand for the whole.",
  "34": "\"Allah commanded, \"Then get out of Paradise, for you are truly cursed.\"\" is the sentence, and it follows immediately upon the explanation.\n\nThe Arabic indicates expulsion, being told to leave and depart. Commentators have discussed what place is meant, whether the garden, the company of the angels, or the elevated station he had occupied. Whatever the precise location, the meaning is exclusion from where he had been.\n\nThe speed of the judgement is worth noticing. There is no further discussion, no attempt to reason with him, and no second chance offered. He had been asked to explain himself, and his explanation confirmed the offence rather than mitigating it.\n\nThat is because of what his answer revealed. A creature who disobeys and regrets it is in a different position from one who disobeys and defends the disobedience as correct. Adam also disobeyed, as the Quran records, and his response was to admit wrong and ask forgiveness, and he was forgiven and guided.\n\nThe difference between the two responses is the entire distinction between a sin and a rebellion.\n\n\"For you are truly cursed.\" names the state he now occupies. The Arabic word indicates being driven far away, expelled and pelted, and it is related to the description used earlier in this chapter for the devils repelled from the sky.\n\nBeing cursed in this sense means being placed permanently outside the reach of mercy. It is not a word shouted in anger but a description of a condition.\n\nThere is something in this account that speaks directly to the audience the chapter was addressing. The Meccan leaders refused a message because of who was delivering it, judging a man by his lack of wealth and standing. The passage shows where that form of reasoning originated and where it leads.\n\nPride is presented here not as one sin among many but as the first refusal, made by a being who never doubted Allah's existence for an instant.",
  "35": "\"\"And surely upon you is condemnation until the Day of Judgment.\" extends the sentence across the whole of time.\n\nThe Arabic indicates a curse that remains upon him, and the duration given is until the Day of Recompense. This is not a temporary exclusion pending review.\n\nCommentators have noted that the mention of that Day does not indicate the curse ends there. It marks the point at which the worldly period closes and the reckoning begins, after which what follows is the punishment itself rather than the curse alone.\n\nThere is a detail worth drawing out about how this sentence relates to what preceded it. Iblis had not been condemned for lacking knowledge, for weakness, or for a momentary failure. He was condemned for refusing and then justifying the refusal, and the permanence of the sentence corresponds to the permanence of his position.\n\nThat correspondence appears throughout the Quran's treatment of consequences. A person who sins and turns back is met with forgiveness, and the Quran and Sunnah are emphatic about this. What is described here is a settled stance maintained without regret, and the outcome matches it exactly.\n\nIt is also worth noticing what Iblis does not do at this point. He does not ask for pardon. Having heard the sentence, his response in the next verse is a request for something else entirely, and the difference is revealing.\n\nA creature in his position might reasonably have asked for another opportunity, or expressed regret, or appealed to his long service. He asks instead for time, and the purpose he intends to use it for is stated in the verses that follow.\n\nFor the reader, this is the most instructive part of the whole account. The refusal to repent is what makes the condition permanent, and the door that remained closed for Iblis was one he never attempted to open.",
  "36": "\"Satan appealed, \"My Lord! Then delay my end until the Day of their resurrection.\"\" is his response to the sentence, and every element of it repays attention.\n\nHe addresses Allah as his Lord. He never denies Him, never disputes His authority, and never questions His right to command. This is a being whose theology, in the narrow sense, is entirely correct and whose refusal has nothing to do with belief.\n\nThat fact alone dismantles a common assumption. Knowing the truth about Allah does not by itself constitute faith, and the Prophet's teaching consistently pairs belief with submission for exactly this reason.\n\nThe request is for delay. He asks not to be finished with until the Day people are raised, which means asking for the longest possible lifespan.\n\nWhat he intends to do with the time is stated in the verses that follow, where he announces his plan to make wrongdoing attractive to human beings and to mislead them all except Allah's sincere servants. The request for life is a request for opportunity.\n\nNotice what he does not ask for. He does not ask for forgiveness, does not express regret, and does not request restoration. The door of repentance was not tried.\n\nThis is the most instructive feature of the whole account for anyone reading it. Iblis was not destroyed by ignorance or by a single act of disobedience. He was destroyed by a refusal to turn back, and the Quran presents no indication that turning back was attempted and rejected.\n\nThere is a warning in this that reaches ordinary human situations. People frequently carry a wrong for years, knowing exactly what it is, while never once attempting the one action that would resolve it. What keeps them there is usually not doubt but the same thing that kept Iblis where he stood.\n\nThe response to his request comes in the next two verses, and its terms are precise.",
  "37": "\"Allah said, \"You will be delayed\"\" grants the request, and the granting raises a question that deserves an honest answer.\n\nWhy would such a request be granted at all? The being making it had just refused a direct command, defended the refusal, and been cursed. He asked for time, and the time was given.\n\nThe answer lies in what this world is for. This chapter and the ones before it have established that human beings are placed here to be tested, and a test requires genuine alternatives. Surah al-Mulk states that death and life were created to test which of us is best in deeds.\n\nA test without any pull in the wrong direction would not be a test. The presence of an adversary who whispers, tempts, and makes wrongdoing look attractive is part of the arrangement that gives human choice its meaning.\n\nThis does not make Iblis a partner in the arrangement or an independent power. He operates entirely within limits set by Allah, and the Quran is explicit that he has no compelling authority over anyone. Surah Ibrahim records him admitting on the Day of Judgement that he had no power over his followers, that he only called them and they responded, and telling them to blame themselves rather than him.\n\nThat admission is the key to understanding this grant of delay. What was given to him was permission to invite, not power to compel.\n\nThere is something worth noticing about the brevity of the answer. No explanation is offered to him, no conditions are attached in this verse, and no reason is given. The reply is two words in the Arabic.\n\nThe limit on the delay comes in the following verse, and it turns out to be considerably less than what he requested, which he appears not to have noticed.",
  "38": "\"\"Until the appointed Day.\" completes the response, and it contains a restriction that is easy to miss.\n\nIblis had asked to be delayed until the Day people are resurrected. What he was granted is delay until the appointed Day, which is not the same thing.\n\nCommentators have drawn attention to this difference. The resurrection is the Day when people are raised from their graves. The appointed Day is understood as the time when the first blowing of the trumpet occurs and all creation dies, which comes earlier.\n\nSo his request was partially refused while appearing to be granted. He asked for existence through to the resurrection; he was given existence until the moment when everything perishes.\n\nThe Arabic phrase indicates a known, determined time. This is the same concept the chapter established at its opening, where it stated that no community is destroyed without a decreed term and that nobody can advance or delay it. The rule that governs nations governs this creature too.\n\nThere is something significant in that. Iblis is presented throughout the Quran as an enemy of enormous persistence, and he is nonetheless subject to a fixed appointment he cannot alter.\n\nThat places his adversarial role in perspective. He is dangerous, and the Quran repeatedly warns against him, describing him as a clear enemy and instructing believers to take him as one. He is also temporary, limited, and operating on a schedule he does not control.\n\nFor a believer, holding both facts at once is the correct position. Underestimating the enemy produces carelessness, and overestimating him produces a kind of fatalism in which every failure is blamed on external influence.\n\nThe same appointment stands over every individual life, not only over the world's end, and the Quran treats each person's death as a fixed hour no less than the trumpet itself. Living toward a deadline he cannot see is the ordinary human condition, and the verse's calm about timetables is meant to be contagious.\n\nThe verses that follow record what he intends to do with the time he has been given, and equally importantly, who he admits he cannot reach.",
  "39": "\"Satan responded, \"My Lord! For allowing me to stray I will surely tempt them on earth\"\" states his intention now that the delay has been granted, and it begins with an accusation.\n\nHe attributes his own straying to Allah, which is a claim worth examining. The Arabic indicates that because Allah left him to go astray, he will do what follows. He is presenting himself as having been placed in this condition rather than having chosen it.\n\nThe account itself refutes him. He was given a command, he refused it, he was asked to explain, and he defended the refusal by ranking himself above the creature he was told to honour. Nothing in the sequence was imposed on him.\n\nThis is a recognisable form of self-justification, and it appears in ordinary human situations constantly. A person who has made a choice and faces its consequences reframes the choice as something that happened to him.\n\nHis stated plan uses a word indicating beautification: he will make things on earth appear attractive. This is the essential method described throughout the Quran. He does not create desires or force actions. He makes what is harmful look appealing, and Surah al-Anfal describes him making their deeds seem pleasing to them.\n\nThat method explains why temptation rarely announces itself as evil. Wrongdoing presents itself as reasonable, deserved, necessary, or harmless, and the presentation is the whole of the work.\n\n\"And mislead them all together\" states the scope of his ambition. Every one of them, without exception.\n\nThe word he uses for leading astray indicates causing someone to lose the way rather than dragging him off it. A guide who gives wrong directions does not carry anyone anywhere; the traveller walks off by himself.\n\nThe exception he immediately names, in the following verse, is the most useful part of the entire declaration for anyone wanting to know how to be protected.",
  "40": "\"\"Except Your chosen servants among them.\" is the exception Satan names himself, and it is the most valuable admission in the passage.\n\nHe has just declared his intention to make things attractive on earth and mislead everyone. Now, unprompted, he concedes that one group is beyond his reach.\n\nThe Arabic term indicates those made sincere, purified and selected. Commentators have noted that the word can be read as describing those who make themselves sincere and those whom Allah has made sincere, and both readings are true of the same people. A person turns toward sincerity, and Allah purifies him.\n\nWhat matters is that the enemy identifies the defence. Anyone wanting to know how to be protected has the answer from the most reliable possible source: the one who is attacking.\n\nSincerity means doing what one does for Allah rather than for any other audience. It is entirely internal, invisible to observers, and it is precisely what temptation cannot get hold of. Satan's method, described in the previous verse, is to make things look attractive, and attraction works on someone who is calculating gain. A person acting purely for Allah has removed the currency in which the offer is made.\n\nThe Prophet warned about the hidden form of associating partners with Allah, which he identified as performing acts for the sake of being seen, in a hadith recorded by Ahmad. The warning exists because sincerity is difficult to maintain, and the difficulty is exactly what makes it protective.\n\nThere is also something in this exchange that should encourage rather than intimidate. Satan's declared scope was everyone; the exception was granted before he was even challenged on it. He knows the limit of what he can do, and the Quran preserves his statement of that limit for the benefit of the people he intends to mislead.\n\nThe response given by Allah in the next verses confirms the boundary and states it as a binding principle.",
  "41": "\"Allah said, \"This is the Way, binding on Me:\" is the reply to Satan's declaration, and its wording carries considerable weight.\n\nThe Arabic indicates a straight path, and the phrase describes it as being upon Allah, meaning He has bound Himself to it. Commentators have explained this in two complementary ways: that the straight path leads to Him, and that maintaining this arrangement is a commitment He has taken upon Himself.\n\nNobody imposes obligations on Allah from outside. What He binds Himself to becomes certain precisely because it comes from Him, and the Quran uses similar language elsewhere, stating that He has taken mercy upon Himself and that saving the believers is a duty He has assumed.\n\nPlaced here, the phrase functions as a guarantee. Satan has announced a plan and named an exception. The response confirms the exception as a fixed rule rather than a concession.\n\nThere is something worth noticing about the tone of this exchange. Satan spoke at length, made accusations, and declared ambitions. The reply is brief and states a principle. There is no argument, no negotiation over terms, and no attempt to dissuade him.\n\nThe image of a path also frames the whole situation in a useful way. A road exists, it leads somewhere, and travellers on it can be called to leave it. Satan's role, as the following verse makes clear, is limited to calling from the roadside.\n\nSurah al-A'raf records him saying he will sit in wait for people on the straight path and come at them from every direction, which is the same picture. He positions himself around a road he cannot close.\n\nFor anyone trying to understand the spiritual situation of a human being, this is the framework the Quran provides: a path that leads to Allah, guaranteed by Him, with an enemy stationed alongside it who has voices but no barriers.",
  "42": "\"\"You will certainly have no authority over My servants\" states the limit directly, and it is one of the most liberating sentences in the Quran.\n\nThe Arabic word indicates compelling authority, the power to force or command. It is denied absolutely. Satan cannot make anyone do anything.\n\nThis is stated repeatedly throughout the Quran, and it culminates in his own admission on the Day of Judgement, recorded in Surah Ibrahim, where he tells his followers that he had no authority over them, that he only called them and they responded, and that they should blame themselves rather than him.\n\nThe practical consequence is significant. Every act a person performs is his own. Temptation is real, whispering is real, and the making of wrongdoing attractive is real, and none of it removes the moment of decision from the person who acts.\n\nThere is a habit of speech that this verse quietly corrects. People describe themselves as having been made to do something, as though an external force had taken control. The Quran does not permit that description, and the enemy himself will not support it.\n\n\"Except the deviant who follow you.\" names the exception, and the direction of the relationship matters.\n\nThey follow him. He does not capture them. The Arabic indicates those who go astray, and their following is described as their own action.\n\nSo even his influence over these people is not authority in the sense just denied. It is the influence a persuasive voice has over someone who has already decided to listen, which is a form of power that depends entirely on the cooperation of its object.\n\nRead together with the previous verses, the passage produces a clear picture. There is a straight path guaranteed by Allah. There is an enemy beside it with a voice and no hands. And there are people who leave the road because they were called, having been given every warning that the call would come.",
  "43": "\"And surely Hell is their destined place, all together.\" states the outcome for those who follow Satan, and it closes the exchange begun several verses earlier.\n\nThe Arabic word indicates a promised place, an appointment fixed in advance. This is not a threat improvised in response to their conduct but a destination already established, which matches the chapter's earlier statements about appointed terms that cannot be moved.\n\nThe phrase \"all together\" is doing important work. Satan and those who followed him arrive at the same place.\n\nThat detail is worth dwelling on, because it exposes the nature of the arrangement they entered. He called, they responded, and there was never a partnership in which one party protects the other. Surah Ibrahim records the moment when this becomes clear to them, where he tells them plainly that he cannot save them and they cannot save him, and that he rejects their having associated him with Allah.\n\nThere is a pattern here that appears in ordinary life on a smaller scale. People are drawn into harmful arrangements by someone who appears to be an ally, and the alliance evaporates precisely when it would be needed. The person who encouraged the wrongdoing is not standing beside anyone at the reckoning.\n\nThe wording also removes an excuse that followers instinctively reach for. Being led does not create a separate, lighter category. The Quran describes the argument that breaks out between leaders and followers in the Fire, with each blaming the other, and both being told they share the punishment.\n\nFor a chapter that has spent so much time on those who mocked messengers and demanded angels, the placement is deliberate. Refusal has a destination, and the verse names it without elaboration.\n\nWhat follows in the next verse describes the structure of that destination, and it introduces a detail about how those who arrive are distributed.",
  "44": "\"It has seven gates, to each a group of them is designated.\" describes the structure of the destination just named, and the detail about gates is worth understanding correctly.\n\nThe Arabic indicates seven entrances, and commentators have explained these as levels or divisions rather than merely doorways. Just as the Quran describes Paradise as having gates and levels, with people entering according to their deeds, the same structure is described here in the opposite direction.\n\nEach gate is assigned a portion of those who arrive. Nobody is placed randomly, and nobody is grouped by accident.\n\nThis tells us something important about divine justice that the Quran states repeatedly. Punishment is proportionate. A person who rejected the truth and spent his life leading others away from it is not treated identically to someone whose wrongdoing was less. Surah Hud stated that the punishment of those who blocked others from Allah's path would be multiplied, which implies exactly the kind of distinction described here.\n\nThe Prophet mentioned in a hadith recorded by Muslim that the hypocrites will be in the lowest depth of the Fire, which the Quran also states in Surah an-Nisa.\n\nThere is a comparison worth making with the description of Paradise in the Quran, which is also described as having gates. In a hadith recorded by al-Bukhari and Muslim, the Prophet described people being called from different gates according to the deeds they were known for, with those who prayed much called from the gate of prayer and those who gave much called from the gate of charity.\n\nThe symmetry in this is instructive. Both destinations are structured, both distribute people according to what they actually did, and neither is a single undifferentiated place.\n\nWhat follows immediately is the other side of the picture, and the chapter turns to it without transition, which is characteristic of how the Quran keeps warning and hope together.",
  "45": "\"Indeed, the righteous will be amid Gardens and springs.\" turns without any transition from the seven gates to the other destination, and the abruptness is deliberate.\n\nThe Quran rarely lets a description of punishment stand alone. Warning is followed by hope, usually immediately, and this pairing runs through the entire Book.\n\nThe Arabic term for the righteous indicates those who guard themselves, and it comes from the root behind \"taqwa,\" the awareness of Allah that makes a person careful about what he does. These are not people described as flawless. They are described as watchful.\n\nTwo features are named, and both would have carried immediate force for the original audience.\n\nGardens meant enclosed, cultivated land with shade and fruit, which in a desert peninsula represented the highest form of settled wealth. The Arabic word for a garden comes from a root meaning to cover or conceal, describing land so densely planted that the ground beneath is hidden.\n\nSprings are the more significant of the two in that setting. A garden depends on water, and in Arabia water was either carried, drawn from wells, or waited for. A spring is water that arrives by itself, continuously, without labour.\n\nPlacing the two together describes abundance that requires no maintenance. Everything a person in that world had to work for is simply present.\n\nThere is a contrast being drawn with the preceding verses that operates at the level of the image itself. The Fire was described as having gates through which people are assigned. Here the description is open ground, gardens, and flowing water, with no gates mentioned at all.\n\nThe vocabulary here ties the Quran's descriptions of Paradise together. Surah ar-Ra'd calls it 'the Paradise promised to the righteous,' with rivers flowing beneath it and fruit and shade that never fail. Water and permanent cultivation reappear wherever the destination of the mindful is described, and the desert imagery never lets the reader forget what it answers.\n\nThe verses that follow develop this further, describing what is said to those who arrive, what is removed from their hearts, and what will never touch them there.",
  "46": "\"˹It will be said to them,˺ \"Enter in peace and security.\"\" is the greeting given at the moment of arrival, and both words in it answer something specific.\n\nThe Arabic \"salam\" indicates safety, freedom from harm, and the absence of anything unpleasant. It is the greeting Muslims exchange daily, and the Quran describes it as the greeting of Paradise. What begins as an ordinary courtesy in this world turns out to be the language spoken at the destination.\n\nThe second word indicates security, being safe from any fear of loss. Commentators have distinguished the two by noting that the first removes any present harm while the second removes any anxiety about the future.\n\nThat combination addresses the two things that undermine every good experience in this life. Something is always slightly wrong, and even when nothing is wrong, there is the knowledge that it will not last.\n\nConsider what the people entering have just come through. The chapter has described the gathering, the reckoning, and the seven gates assigned to those who arrive at the other destination. For anyone who has passed through that, being told to enter in peace and security is the news that matters more than any description of gardens.\n\nThere is also something in the word \"enter\" that is worth noticing. They are invited rather than admitted, welcomed rather than processed. The Quran describes angels greeting the people of Paradise at the gates, saying peace be upon them, and telling them to enter and remain.\n\nThe same greeting appeared in Surah ar-Ra'd, where angels entered from every gate saying peace be upon them for their perseverance, and in Surah Ibrahim, where the people of the Gardens are greeted with peace.\n\nThe repetition across chapters establishes something about the character of that place. Of everything that could be emphasised, the Quran keeps returning to safety and to a word spoken between people.",
  "47": "\"We will remove whatever bitterness they had in their hearts.\" describes something that happens to the people themselves rather than to their surroundings, and it may be the most remarkable detail in the entire description.\n\nThe Arabic word indicates rancour, ill-feeling, the residue of resentment that settles in a chest. It is not describing open hostility but the quieter thing that remains after a dispute is over: coolness, remembered slights, the small reservation a person holds toward someone who once wronged him.\n\nAlmost everyone carries some of this. Family disagreements that never fully healed, the memory of being humiliated, competition with someone who did better. Even good people, living decent lives, arrive at old age with a certain amount of it lodged somewhere.\n\nThe verse says it will be taken out. Not suppressed or forgiven through effort, but removed.\n\nThis addresses something that would otherwise be a genuine problem. A place of perfect peace populated by people who remember every grievance would not be perfect. The removal is what makes the gathering possible.\n\nThere is a report explained in the hadith literature describing believers being purified before entry so that nothing remains between them, and a well-known statement from Ali ibn Abi Talib, mentioned in the commentaries, expressing hope that he and others would be among those the verse describes.\n\n\"In a friendly manner, they will be on thrones, facing one another.\" completes the picture, and the detail about facing is the point.\n\nPeople sitting face to face are people with nothing to hide and no reason to avoid a gaze. In this world, seating arrangements at gatherings are quietly political, and people position themselves to avoid those they would rather not deal with.\n\nNone of that operates there, because the thing that produced it has been removed. What remains is company, which the Quran presents as part of the reward rather than a background detail.",
  "48": "\"No fatigue will touch them there\" names the first of two things absent from that place, and it is easy to underestimate.\n\nThe Arabic word indicates weariness, exhaustion, the state of being worn out by effort. It covers physical tiredness and the deeper depletion that comes from sustained strain.\n\nEvery good experience in this life is limited by this. A person can only enjoy something for as long as his body and attention hold out. Pleasures end not because they stop being available but because the person becomes tired of them, and this happens with food, with company, with travel, and with every other enjoyment.\n\nRemoving fatigue changes the nature of enjoyment itself. What is described is not a place where good things are available but a state in which the capacity to receive them never diminishes.\n\nThere is a related statement in the Quran, in Surah Fatir, where the people of Paradise praise Allah for settling them in a home of permanence where no fatigue touches them and no weariness.\n\n\"Nor will they ever be asked to leave.\" names the second absence, and it addresses a different anxiety.\n\nThe Arabic indicates being expelled or made to go out. Nobody will be removed.\n\nThis matters because every good situation in this world is held on temporary terms. A person enjoys health until it fails, a home until circumstances change, and company until people move or die. Beneath every pleasant experience runs a low awareness that it is borrowed.\n\nThe Quran repeatedly emphasises permanence when describing Paradise, and the reason becomes clear when the two absences in this verse are read together. Enjoyment is limited by exhaustion and by ending, and both are removed at once.\n\nWhat is left is the only condition in which happiness could be complete, which is precisely why the chapter has been urging people toward it.",
  "49": "\"Inform My servants ˹O Prophet˺ that I am truly the All-Forgiving, Most Merciful,\" is an instruction to deliver a message, and the message is about Allah Himself.\n\nThe two names are among the most frequently paired in the Quran. The first indicates extensive, repeated forgiveness, covering what has been done. The second indicates continuous mercy that reaches those who receive it.\n\nNotice who is being addressed. They are called My servants, which is a term of relationship rather than accusation. The verse does not open by naming their failures.\n\nThe instruction to inform is worth pausing over. This is not presented as information people naturally possess. It has to be told to them, which suggests that human beings do not arrive at an accurate view of Allah's mercy on their own.\n\nThat observation holds up. People tend to construct a picture of God from their own experience of authority, and human authority is generally conditional, easily offended, and slow to forgive. A person who has been treated that way by parents, employers, or communities projects the same pattern upward.\n\nThe Prophet corrected this repeatedly, and the hadith literature is full of it. He described Allah as more pleased with a servant's repentance than a man who loses his camel with all his provisions in a desert and then finds it again, in a report recorded by al-Bukhari and Muslim.\n\nThere is also something significant about the placement of this verse. It comes immediately after the descriptions of Paradise, at the point where a reader might feel that such a reward belongs to people better than himself.\n\nDespair is what the verse is aimed at, and the chapter will state this explicitly a few verses later when Abraham asks who despairs of the mercy of his Lord except those who have gone astray.\n\nThe balance to this announcement follows immediately in the next verse.",
  "50": "\"And that My torment is indeed the most painful.\" completes the message begun in the previous verse, and the pairing is the whole point.\n\nThe Prophet was instructed to inform people of two things, and neither is delivered without the other. Forgiveness and mercy were announced first; the severity of the punishment is announced second, in the same breath and as part of the same instruction.\n\nThe Arabic indicates the painful punishment, and the construction gives it emphasis.\n\nThis pairing runs through the entire Quran, and it exists because either half taken alone produces a distortion.\n\nA picture of Allah built only from mercy produces carelessness. If forgiveness is automatic and unconditional regardless of conduct, then warnings mean nothing, injustice has no consequence, and the whole moral structure collapses. People who hold this view tend to postpone repentance indefinitely, since nothing appears to be at stake.\n\nA picture built only from severity produces despair. A person who believes he is dealing with an authority looking for reasons to punish will eventually stop trying, and the Quran treats that despair as a serious spiritual failure rather than as humility.\n\nThe Prophet expressed the balance in a hadith recorded by Muslim, saying that if a believer knew the extent of Allah's punishment, nobody would hope for His Paradise, and if a disbeliever knew the extent of His mercy, nobody would despair of it.\n\nScholars have long described the believer's state as flying between two wings: hope and fear. Losing either one makes flight impossible.\n\nThere is something instructive in the order used here. Mercy came first. That order appears consistently in the Quran, and the Prophet reported that Allah wrote a decree about Himself which is with Him above the Throne, stating that His mercy prevails over His wrath, in a hadith recorded by al-Bukhari.",
  "51": "\"And inform them ˹O Prophet˺ about Abraham's guests\" begins an account that illustrates the two announcements just made.\n\nThe instruction is the same verb used in the preceding verses. He was told to inform people of Allah's forgiveness and mercy, and of the severity of His punishment, and now he is told to inform them about this episode.\n\nThat connection is the reason the story appears here. What follows contains both halves in a single sequence. The same visitors who bring news of a son to an old man are on their way to destroy a city, and the account moves from one to the other without pause.\n\nThe guests were angels in human form, which is why Abraham did not recognise them. This chapter has already mentioned that angels are not sent down except for a decisive matter, and this visit was exactly that.\n\nAbraham appears throughout the Quran as the model of hospitality, and Surah adh-Dhariyat gives fuller detail of this same visit, describing how he slipped away to his household and returned with a fattened calf, and how he offered it to them without any announcement of his effort.\n\nThe word for these visitors indicates guests, people received into a home. The whole episode is framed in terms of hospitality before anything else is mentioned, which reflects the value Islam places on it. The Prophet said that whoever believes in Allah and the Last Day should honour his guest, in a hadith recorded by al-Bukhari and Muslim.\n\nSurah Hud narrated this same visit, and the present account emphasises different aspects of it. There the focus fell on Abraham's fear at the untouched food and his pleading for Lot's people. Here the emphasis will fall on the exchange about despair and hope, which suits the message the Prophet had just been instructed to convey.",
  "52": "\"Who entered upon him and greeted ˹him with˺, \"Peace!\"\" describes the arrival, and the greeting is the same word that appeared a few verses earlier in the welcome given to the people of Paradise.\n\nThe greeting of peace is the practice of the prophets, and the Quran preserves Abraham's exchange with these visitors as one of its earliest recorded instances. Muslims continue it today, which connects an ordinary daily habit to this scene.\n\n\"He ˹later˺ said, \"Surely we are afraid of you.\"\" reports his reaction, and the fuller version of the episode explains why.\n\nSurah Hud describes what happened between the greeting and this statement. Abraham prepared a roasted calf and offered it, and his guests did not reach for it. In the customs of that world, refusing a host's food was a serious signal, since sharing a meal created an obligation of protection between host and guest. Visitors who would not eat might have come with hostile intent.\n\nHis fear was therefore reasonable rather than superstitious. He was reading the situation correctly according to everything he knew.\n\nThere is something worth noticing in the Quran recording a prophet's fear openly. Prophets in the Quran are never presented as beings without ordinary human reactions. Moses felt fear when his staff moved, and Lot was distressed when his visitors arrived in a dangerous city. What distinguishes them is not the absence of fear but their conduct while feeling it.\n\nNotice also that Abraham states his fear rather than concealing it. He tells them directly that they frighten him, which is a form of honesty that many people would avoid in front of strangers.\n\nThe response he receives in the next verse follows the pattern used whenever angels address human beings in the Quran. They calm him first, and only then deliver the news, which is a sequence worth noticing for anyone who has to deliver something unexpected.",
  "53": "\"They reassured ˹him˺, \"Do not be afraid!\"\" is the first thing the visitors say, and the order matters.\n\nThey calm him before informing him. This pattern appears consistently in the Quran whenever angels address a human being, and it reflects something about how unexpected news should be delivered. A person who is frightened cannot absorb information, and the fear has to be dealt with before anything else can be communicated.\n\n\"Surely we give you good news of a knowledgeable son.\" is the announcement itself, and every part of it is significant.\n\nThe Arabic word indicates glad tidings, news that produces joy. The same root gives the word for the effect such news has on a person's face.\n\nThe son promised is Isaac, as Surah Hud makes explicit in its account of this same visit, where the news is given to Abraham's wife and extends to a grandson, Jacob, after him.\n\nThe description of the son as knowledgeable is worth pausing over. Of everything that could have been promised about this child, what is mentioned is knowledge. Not wealth, not strength, not a long life. Isaac became a prophet, and the line descending from him produced Jacob, Joseph, Moses, David, Solomon, and Jesus.\n\nThis says something about what the Quran treats as the highest thing a person can be given. A child described in advance as knowledgeable is being described as someone who will know Allah and carry His message, which is the only form of knowledge the Quran consistently praises without qualification.\n\nAbraham's reaction, recorded in the next verse, is astonishment rather than immediate joy, and that reaction is understandable given his circumstances. He and his wife had grown old without children by her, and the announcement overturned everything they had come to accept about their situation.\n\nWhat follows is one of the Quran's clearest exchanges about hope and despair.",
  "54": "\"He wondered, \"Do you give me good news despite my old age?\"\" is Abraham's response, and it is entirely human.\n\nHe is not rejecting the news or doubting Allah's power. He is expressing astonishment at something that overturns everything he had come to accept about his situation.\n\nThe circumstances make the reaction understandable. He and Sarah had grown old, and Surah Hud records her reaction to the same announcement in similar terms, striking her face and describing herself as a barren old woman.\n\n\"What unlikely news!\" completes his response with an exclamation. The Arabic indicates asking what kind of good news this could be, and the tone conveys wonder rather than refusal.\n\nThere is something valuable in the Quran preserving this reaction rather than presenting a prophet receiving extraordinary news with serene composure. Scripture could easily have recorded only the acceptance. Instead it records the shock, which makes the account both honest and useful.\n\nThe reaction also tells us how long they had waited and how completely they had adjusted to their circumstances. People accommodate themselves to disappointment. After enough years, a hope stops being examined and becomes a settled fact about life.\n\nThat accommodation is precisely what the announcement disturbed, and it explains the answer he receives in the next verse. The angels do not simply confirm the news; they warn him against despair, which suggests they recognised something in his response that needed addressing.\n\nThere is a distinction worth making here that the passage itself will develop. Being astonished at good news is not despair, and Abraham was not guilty of it. What the following verses do is take a moment of understandable surprise and use it to state a principle about hope that applies to everyone.\n\nThe reply he receives, and his own answer to it, form one of the most quoted exchanges in the Quran on this subject.",
  "55": "\"They responded, \"We give you good news in all truth, so do not be one of those who despair.\"\" is the angels' reply, and it does two things at once.\n\nFirst it confirms the announcement. The Arabic indicates that the good news is given with the truth, meaning it is certain and settled rather than a possibility being raised. This is not an encouragement to hope for something; it is information about what will happen.\n\nSecond, and more significantly, it warns him against despair.\n\nThat warning is worth examining, because Abraham had not expressed despair. He had expressed astonishment, which the previous verse recorded honestly. The angels are not accusing him; they are closing off a road before he reaches it.\n\nThere is a difference between the two states that the passage is careful about. Surprise is a reaction to news. Despair is a settled conclusion that nothing will change, and it operates before any news arrives. A person who has despaired has stopped asking, stopped expecting, and often stopped noticing possibilities.\n\nThe Arabic word indicates cutting off hope entirely, and the Quran treats it as a serious matter rather than a mood. Jacob told his sons not to lose hope in Allah's mercy, adding that nobody despairs of it except those with no faith. Surah az-Zumar instructs those who have wronged themselves greatly not to despair of Allah's mercy, since He forgives all sins.\n\nThe connection to the message the Prophet had just been instructed to deliver is direct. He was told a few verses earlier to inform people that Allah is the All-Forgiving, the Most Merciful. This episode illustrates what accepting that announcement actually requires.\n\nAbraham's answer in the next verse shows that he understood exactly what was being said to him, and his reply has become one of the Quran's standard statements on the subject.",
  "56": "\"He exclaimed, \"Who would despair of the mercy of their Lord except the misguided?\"\" is Abraham's reply, and it clarifies his earlier reaction completely.\n\nHe was never despairing. His astonishment was at the overturning of natural expectation, not a doubt about what Allah could do, and his answer makes that unmistakable.\n\nThe response takes the form of a question that answers itself. Nobody in his right mind despairs of Allah's mercy, and anyone who does has gone astray.\n\nThe Arabic word for those who despair here indicates cutting off hope completely, and the word for the misguided indicates those who have wandered from the path. Abraham is connecting the two: despair is not merely a sad state but a symptom of being lost.\n\nThe reasoning behind that connection is worth drawing out, because despair is often mistaken for humility. A person who says he is beyond help may believe he is being realistic about his own failures.\n\nThe Quran treats it differently. Despairing of Allah's mercy contains a hidden claim about Allah: that His mercy has limits, that His power has run out, or that a person's situation exceeds His capacity to change it. Stated plainly, nobody would defend any of these propositions, and yet despair asserts them in practice.\n\nJacob made the identical point in Surah Yusuf, telling his sons to go and search for Joseph and not to lose hope in Allah's mercy, since nobody despairs of it except the disbelieving people. He said this as a blind old man who had lost two sons and had no evidence that either was alive.\n\nTwo prophets, in different chapters and different situations, reach the same conclusion from opposite ends of experience: one who had lost everything and one who had just been given more than he expected.\n\nThe passage now turns, and Abraham asks the question that reveals why the angels had really come.",
  "57": "\"He ˹then˺ added, \"What is your mission, O messenger-angels?\"\" is Abraham's question once the good news has been delivered and accepted.\n\nBy now he knows who they are. The greeting, the untouched food, and the announcement of a son have made their identity clear, and he addresses them as messengers.\n\nThe question itself shows something about his understanding. He recognises that angels are not sent for a single errand of good news, and this chapter has already stated the principle: they are not sent down except for a decisive matter.\n\nSo Abraham asks what else. He knows there is more, and he wants to know what it is.\n\nThere is a quality of mind visible in this that the Quran presents without comment. A man has just been told he will have a son in his old age, which is news enough to occupy anyone completely. He absorbs it, and then he asks about the rest of the business.\n\nThe Arabic word he uses for their mission indicates their affair, the matter they have come about, and the form of address he uses is respectful.\n\nNotice also what his question implies about his concern. He is not asking out of curiosity about angelic affairs. Surah Hud, narrating this same visit, shows what he did with the answer once he received it: he began pleading for the people of Lot, arguing on behalf of a city about to be destroyed.\n\nThat reaction is the reason the Quran describes him in that chapter as forbearing, tender-hearted, and constantly turning to his Lord.\n\nThe answer he receives in the next verse is brief, and it identifies a people rather than a place, which is characteristic of how the Quran describes such judgements. Communities are destroyed for what they became, not for where they lived.",
  "58": "\"They replied, \"We have actually been sent to a wicked people.\"\" is the answer, and it is deliberately brief.\n\nNo city is named at this point, no crimes are listed, and no details are given. The people are identified by what they had become.\n\nThe Arabic word indicates criminals, those who have cut themselves off from what is right through deliberate wrongdoing. It is a term the Quran uses throughout for people whose conduct has moved past occasional failure into an established way of being.\n\nThe people in question are those of Lot, as the following verses make clear. The Quran describes their conduct in several places: assaulting travellers, committing openly what no community had done before them, and doing wrong in their public gatherings.\n\nWhat is worth noticing in the angels' answer is what it does not do. It does not describe the offences in detail, does not express satisfaction at the coming judgement, and does not invite Abraham to approve.\n\nHis reaction, recorded in Surah Hud, was to begin pleading for them. That reaction is remarkable when read alongside this verse, because he had just been told they were criminals. He argued for them anyway, pointing out that Lot was among them, until he was told that the decree had already come and the matter was closed.\n\nThe placement of this account in this particular chapter serves a purpose. The Prophet had been instructed a few verses earlier to inform people of two things: Allah's forgiveness and mercy, and the severity of His punishment. This episode contains both, delivered by the same visitors on the same day.\n\nA child is promised to an old man who had given up expecting one, and a city is scheduled for destruction. The mercy and the severity are not competing messages; they are two aspects of a single arrangement, and the chapter presents them together deliberately.",
  "59": "\"As for the family of Lot, we will certainly deliver them all,\" is the exception the angels add immediately after describing the people they were sent to.\n\nThe Arabic emphasises certainty, and the phrase indicates the whole of them, without loss. Before any destruction is mentioned in detail, the rescue is stated.\n\nThis ordering appears consistently in the Quran's accounts of destroyed communities. Surah Hud narrated the endings of Noah's people, Ad, Thamud, and Midian, and in each case the deliverance of the believers was mentioned before the punishment of the rest.\n\nThe pattern reflects something about the purpose of these accounts. They are not primarily records of who was destroyed. They are demonstrations that Allah does not lose those who hold to Him, however small their number and however hostile their surroundings.\n\nLot's household was tiny. He had been sent to a large, settled population and had spent years calling them, and what he had at the end was his own family, minus one member.\n\nThere is something worth noticing in the way the angels speak here. They had described the people of the city as criminals in a single phrase, without elaboration. Now they describe the family in terms of what will be done for them.\n\nThe Arabic word for the family covers a household, those attached to a person by kinship and by living together. In the Quran's usage it frequently indicates those who follow a person in his religion as well as those related to him, which becomes relevant immediately, since the next verse names an exception within the household.\n\nThat exception is what makes this verse's promise precise rather than sentimental. Deliverance is not extended by association, and the following verse states this without softening it, which prevents the promise from being misread as protection for anyone who happens to live in the right house.",
  "60": "\"Except his wife. We have determined that she will be one of the doomed.\" is the exception, and it removes any suggestion that rescue works by association.\n\nLot's wife lived in the house of a prophet. She shared his home, his meals, and his daily life, and she was not saved.\n\nThe reason is given in Surah at-Tahrim, which presents her alongside Noah's wife as an example concerning those who disbelieve. Both were married to righteous servants of Allah, both betrayed them, and neither husband could avail them anything against Allah. Commentators explain her betrayal as informing the men of the city about Lot's guests, which the events of this very night would have involved.\n\nThe principle established here runs throughout the Quran. Noah's son drowned. Abraham's father refused. Lot's wife was left behind. Proximity to a prophet transfers nothing.\n\nThat principle deserves emphasis, because religious communities in every generation drift toward the opposite assumption. People rely on descent, on membership, on association with the pious, and on being part of the right group. The Prophet addressed this directly with his own relatives, telling them plainly that he could not avail them anything against Allah, in a hadith recorded by al-Bukhari and Muslim.\n\nThe Arabic phrase indicates that this outcome had been determined, and commentators understood it as a decree based on what she had chosen and persisted in, consistent with what the Quran states everywhere about people being dealt with according to their own conduct.\n\nThere is a hard implication in this verse for families. A person can raise children in a household of prayer and lose one of them, and a righteous person can be married to someone who never accepts what he stands for.\n\nThe Quran records such cases openly rather than presenting faith as something that flows automatically through a home, and Lot is about to spend the most difficult night of his life without knowing this detail.",
  "61": "\"So when the messengers came to the family of Lot,\" moves the scene from Abraham's household to a city about to be destroyed.\n\nThe transition is abrupt in the text. One moment the visitors are explaining their mission over an untouched meal; the next they have arrived at their destination.\n\nCalling them messengers here rather than guests is significant. In Abraham's house they were received as visitors, and the language reflected hospitality. Here they are identified by their function, which is what they have come to carry out.\n\nThe distance between the two households would have been considerable, and the Quran passes over the journey entirely. What matters is the arrival.\n\nLot's situation at this point is worth recalling. He was Abraham's nephew and had migrated with him before being sent as a prophet to the cities of the plain, in the region associated with the area near the Dead Sea. He had spent years calling a population whose conduct the Quran describes as unprecedented, and he had made no progress with them.\n\nHe was also, in a practical sense, alone. Surah Hud records his cry that he wished he had the strength to resist them or a strong supporter to rely on, and the Prophet Muhammad commented on that moment with sympathy, noting that Lot was seeking support in a strong pillar.\n\nWhat Lot did not know as the visitors arrived was that the strongest possible support had just walked through his door. Surah Hud describes his distress at their arrival, since he saw only male visitors of striking appearance entering a city known for assaulting travellers.\n\nHis reaction, recorded in the next verse, is the response of a man who cannot see what is actually happening, which is a situation the Quran presents without any suggestion that his fear was foolish.",
  "62": "\"He said, \"You are surely an unfamiliar people!\"\" is Lot's greeting, and it is not a warm one.\n\nThe Arabic indicates people who are unknown, unrecognised, strangers. Commentators have discussed the tone, and most understood it as expressing unease rather than curiosity. Some read it as an expression of alarm at their appearance in his city.\n\nHis reaction makes complete sense given what he knew. Surah Hud records that he was distressed and troubled by their arrival and said it was a terrible day, because he understood immediately what his people would attempt against visitors.\n\nThere is something worth noticing about the position this places him in. Hospitality was among the highest obligations in that world, and Lot's uncle Abraham had just demonstrated it perfectly, slipping away to prepare a fattened calf for the same visitors without announcing his effort.\n\nLot could not do the same. His difficulty was not that he lacked generosity but that receiving guests in his city was dangerous for the guests, which is a measure of how far the place had fallen. A society in which a decent man cannot safely offer shelter has lost something more basic than any single moral rule.\n\nThe word for strangers also carries a practical implication. Their unfamiliarity is exactly what put them at risk, since the men of the city targeted travellers passing through.\n\nLot's statement therefore functions partly as a warning. He is telling them they will be noticed.\n\nWhat he does not know is that these visitors were sent precisely because of what the city does to strangers, and that his own household is the reason they have come rather than the vulnerability he imagines.\n\nTheir response in the next verse begins to correct his understanding of the situation, and it does so by identifying themselves in terms of the thing his people had refused to take seriously.",
  "63": "\"They responded, \"We have come to you with that ˹torment˺ which they have doubted.\"\" is the visitors' answer, and it identifies them by their mission rather than by their nature.\n\nThe Arabic indicates that they have brought what the people were disputing about, treating as uncertain, refusing to accept as real.\n\nThat is a precise description of what had been happening. Lot had warned his people for years, and the warning was met with doubt rather than argument. Surah al-Qamar records them treating his warnings as a matter to dispute, and the Quran elsewhere describes them challenging him to bring on Allah's punishment if he was telling the truth.\n\nNow it has arrived, carried by the men standing in his doorway.\n\nThere is something worth pausing over in the way the answer is framed. The angels do not begin by describing their own identity or reassuring Lot about the guests' safety. They describe what they have brought, and they connect it directly to the doubt of the people outside.\n\nThat connection matters for the chapter's argument. This surah opened with people mocking the Prophet, calling him insane, and demanding that angels be sent down as proof. The chapter answered that angels are not sent except for a decisive matter, and that if they came, the disbelievers would not be given any delay.\n\nHere is the demonstration of exactly that principle. A community doubted a warning, demanded proof, and eventually received the angels they had implicitly been asking for. The visit ended the city the same morning.\n\nThe Meccans hearing this account were being shown what their own demand actually meant, without needing anyone to spell it out.\n\nTheir wording also settles a question about timing. The torment was not a sudden, arbitrary act; it arrived after years of warning, preaching, and open challenges from the people themselves to bring it on. Doubt had been given every opportunity to turn into belief, and the record shows that it refused each one.\n\nThe reassurance Lot needed comes in the following verse, along with a statement about truthfulness that answers years of accusation against him.",
  "64": "\"\"We come to you with the truth, and we are certainly truthful.\" is the second half of the visitors' answer, and it emphasises truth twice in a single sentence.\n\nThe repetition is deliberate. They have come with the truth, and they themselves are truthful.\n\nThe first phrase concerns what they carry. The Arabic word indicates what is real, established, and rightly so. The punishment is not a possibility or a threat that might be reconsidered; it is a settled reality being delivered.\n\nThe second phrase concerns them as messengers. They are stating that their report can be relied upon.\n\nBoth statements land in a particular context, and the context is what gives them force. Lot had spent years being disbelieved. His people treated his warnings as doubtful, and Surah ash-Shu'ara records them threatening to expel him. A man in that position has grown accustomed to his word carrying no weight with anyone.\n\nNow he is being addressed by visitors who open by insisting on their own truthfulness, which is precisely the quality his people had denied him.\n\nThere is also something in this for the chapter's wider argument. The surah opened by describing the Quran as the clear Book, and it has been dealing throughout with people who treated a clear message as doubtful. The word for truth appears here in the same way it appears in the chapter's opening description of revelation.\n\nFor Lot personally, the effect of this statement would have been immediate relief mixed with something heavier. The danger to his guests evaporates in a sentence, and in the same sentence he learns that the city he has lived in and called for years is finished.\n\nWhat follows is the instruction for his departure, given with precise details about timing, order, and one prohibition that has been the subject of much discussion.",
  "65": "\"\"So travel with your family in the dark of night, and follow ˹closely˺ behind them.\" gives Lot his instructions, and every detail has a practical purpose.\n\nTravelling at night provided cover. A household leaving a hostile city in daylight would be seen and stopped. The same method appears in the migration of the Prophet Muhammad from Mecca, and in the departure of Moses with the Children of Israel.\n\nThe instruction to follow behind them places Lot at the rear of his own family. A leader walking last can see everyone ahead of him, notice anyone falling behind, and keep the group moving. This is the position of responsibility rather than privilege, and it is worth noticing that a prophet is told to take it.\n\n\"Do not let any of you look back\" is the prohibition, and commentators have explained it in complementary ways. Looking back would slow the group and create hesitation. It would also mean witnessing a punishment they were being spared from, and there is a further sense of not being drawn back emotionally toward what was being left behind.\n\nThat last dimension is the one with the widest application. A person leaving something harmful is often defeated not by the difficulty of leaving but by looking back at it. Attachment survives departure, and the instruction addresses exactly that.\n\n\"And go where you are commanded.\" is the final element, and it leaves the destination unstated.\n\nLot is told to move in the direction he has been given without being told where the journey ends. He is required to trust the instruction rather than the plan.\n\nThat is a demanding position for anyone leading a family through the night out of a city about to be destroyed, and it is the same demand made of Abraham when he left his wife and infant son in a barren valley, and of Moses when he struck the sea with nothing visible ahead of him.",
  "66": "\"We revealed to him this decree: \"Those ˹sinners˺ will be uprooted in the morning.\"\" states the timing of what was coming, and the certainty of it.\n\nThe Arabic word rendered as decree indicates a matter decided and settled. This is not a warning of what might happen but information about what has been determined.\n\nThe image used is striking. The Arabic indicates that their root will be cut off, severed at the base. It is the language of removing a plant entirely rather than pruning it, and it conveys complete elimination with nothing left to grow back.\n\nThe Quran uses similar imagery elsewhere for destroyed peoples. Surah Hud described the evil word as an evil tree uprooted from the earth with no stability, and it described the end of Thamud and Midian with the phrase that it was as if they had never lived there.\n\n\"In the morning\" fixes the time, and this detail matters for what follows. Lot now knows exactly how long he has. Surah Hud records the angels telling him that their appointed time is the morning, and adding a question: is the morning not near?\n\nThat question carries a particular weight for a man who had just spent years waiting. The whole span of his mission was about to close within hours.\n\nThere is also something in the choice of the morning as the hour. It is the point at which a city wakes and ordinary life resumes, which is precisely when these people were removed. Surah al-Qamar records that a lasting punishment came upon them in the early morning.\n\nThe Quran repeatedly describes destruction arriving at moments of routine rather than at moments of crisis. Surah Yunus asked whether people had considered that His torment might come by night or by day, and pointed out that the wicked do not realise what they are asking to be hastened.",
  "67": "\"And there came the men of the city, rejoicing.\" describes the arrival of the crowd, and the final word is the one that makes the verse disturbing.\n\nThey came rejoicing. The Arabic indicates delight and pleasure, the mood of people anticipating something enjoyable.\n\nNews of strangers in Lot's house had spread, and the reaction of the population was celebration. Surah Hud describes them rushing toward him, and notes that they were already accustomed to committing evil deeds.\n\nWhat the Quran captures in this single word is the stage a society reaches when wrongdoing has stopped being furtive. There was no shame in their approach, no attempt at concealment, and no sense that they were doing anything requiring privacy. They came as a group, openly, and in good spirits.\n\nThat openness is the real measure of the city's condition. Individual wrongdoing exists in every society, and Islam addresses it with repentance and concealment of faults. What is described here is different: a community in which the wrong had become the normal entertainment, publicly pursued and collectively enjoyed.\n\nThe Quran notes elsewhere that they used to commit their offences openly in their gatherings, which fits this scene exactly.\n\nThere is also a contrast being drawn between two households on the same night. In one, an old man had hurried to prepare a calf for strangers without announcing his effort. In the other, a city hurried toward strangers with entirely different intentions.\n\nThe visitors were the same in both cases.\n\nFor Lot, this was the moment he had been dreading since the guests arrived, and Surah Hud records his distress at their coming and his statement that it was a terrible day.\n\nHis response, recorded in the next verses, is a plea addressed to men he had spent years warning, and it appeals to the last standard he thinks they might still recognise.",
  "68": "\"Lot pleaded, \"Indeed, these are my guests, so do not embarrass me.\"\" is his first attempt to stop the crowd, and it appeals to a standard he hopes they still recognise.\n\nThe word for guests is doing the work here. In that world, and across the Near East generally, hospitality carried an almost sacred weight. A guest under a man's roof was under his protection, and violating that protection disgraced the host permanently.\n\nLot is invoking the last shared value he can find. He does not begin by arguing about right and wrong in general, which he had been doing for years without effect. He appeals to something concrete and social.\n\nThe Arabic indicates asking them not to disgrace him, not to expose him to shame. He is framing the matter in terms of what their action would do to him, which is a way of making an abstract wrong immediate and personal.\n\nThere is something worth noticing about the position he is in. A prophet is standing at his own door negotiating with a crowd. He has no authority over them, no force to call on, and no allies, and Surah Hud preserves his wish for strength or a strong supporter.\n\nWhat he does have is his standing as a member of the community and the obligation of hospitality, and he uses both.\n\nIt is also worth noticing what he does not do. He does not hand the guests over, does not attempt to save himself by cooperating, and does not close the door and let events take their course elsewhere. He stands in front of them and argues.\n\nThat is the conduct of someone whose principles hold under pressure rather than in comfortable conditions, which is the only real test of principles.\n\nHis second appeal, in the following verse, moves from the social obligation to the higher one, and it names the standard that should have settled the matter before any of this began.",
  "69": "\"\"Fear Allah and do not humiliate me.\" is Lot's second appeal, and it moves from social obligation to the highest standard available.\n\nThe order of his two appeals is worth noticing. He began with hospitality, which was a value his people still nominally recognised. Now he raises the matter to where it actually belongs.\n\nThe Arabic phrase indicates guarding oneself in relation to Allah, being conscious of Him and careful because of that consciousness. It is the same root behind \"taqwa,\" and it appears throughout the Quran as the foundation of right conduct.\n\nWhat makes this appeal remarkable is that Lot is addressing people who had rejected everything he had told them about Allah for years. He tries it anyway.\n\nThere is something instructive in that persistence. A person calling others to good is often tempted to abandon the strongest argument because it has already been refused, and to rely instead on whatever seems more likely to persuade. Lot uses both, and he does not drop the higher one because the lower one seemed more practical.\n\n\"And do not humiliate me\" repeats the personal appeal from the previous verse, using a different word. The Arabic indicates disgracing someone, bringing him low in front of others.\n\nHe is telling them what their action would cost him personally. Whatever else they thought of his message, they had known him for years as a man living among them.\n\nThe pairing of the two appeals in this verse captures the whole situation. He asks them to consider Allah, and he asks them to consider him. Neither succeeded.\n\nTheir answer, in the next verse, ignores both entirely and reveals that they had already made his position clear to him before this night. It turns out that protecting anyone had itself become forbidden in that city, which tells us more about the place than any description of their conduct could.",
  "70": "\"They responded, \"Have we not forbidden you from protecting anyone?\"\" is the crowd's answer, and it reveals something about the city that no description of their conduct could convey as efficiently.\n\nThey had already banned him from offering protection. The Arabic indicates that they had forbidden him from taking in or defending people, and the phrasing suggests this was an established prohibition rather than a new demand.\n\nConsider what that means about the place. Sheltering a stranger had been made an offence. A man was not permitted to stand between a traveller and the crowd, and Lot had evidently been warned about this before.\n\nThat single detail explains the scale of what had gone wrong there. Individual wrongdoing existed in every ancient city. What is described here is a society that had organised itself against decency, making the protection of the vulnerable something one could be prohibited from doing.\n\nTheir reply also ignores both of his appeals completely. He had asked them to fear Allah and not to disgrace him, and they answer with a procedural objection about a rule he had broken.\n\nThere is something recognisable in that form of response. A moral appeal is met with a technicality, and the technicality allows the crowd to feel that Lot is the one in the wrong. He is the rule-breaker; they are enforcing an agreed position.\n\nThis is how communities that have lost their bearings usually operate. They do not announce that they have abandoned right and wrong. They construct rules that make wrongdoing procedurally correct and decency a violation.\n\nSurah ash-Shu'ara records a further stage of the same pressure, where they threatened to expel him from the city if he did not stop, and he replied that he detested their conduct.\n\nHis final attempt, in the next verse, offers them a lawful alternative, which is the last thing he has left to try.",
  "71": "\"He said, \"O my people! Here are my daughters ˹so marry them˺ if you wish to do so.\"\" is Lot's final attempt, and it requires careful explanation because it is easily misread.\n\nHe is offering marriage, not handing over women to a crowd. The Arabic indicates that these are available to them lawfully, and Surah Hud makes the point explicit by adding that they are pure for them, meaning lawful.\n\nCommentators have understood the reference in two ways. Many took it as his own daughters, offered in lawful marriage. Others, including a number of early authorities, explained that a prophet stands as a father to his community, so his daughters means the women of the town available for marriage.\n\nEither reading leads to the same point. He is directing them toward what Allah permitted and away from what they intended.\n\nNotice also how he addresses them: \"O my people.\" After years of rejection, threats, and a crowd at his door, he still speaks to them as his own community. This form of address appears throughout the prophetic accounts in the Quran, and it reflects a consistent quality in these men. Correction was offered from within a relationship rather than shouted from outside it.\n\nThe desperation of the offer should not be missed either. This is a man who has exhausted every argument. He appealed to hospitality, to fear of Allah, and to his own dignity, and none of it worked. What remains is to place a lawful alternative directly in front of them.\n\nThat the offer failed is clear from Surah Hud, which records their reply that he knew they had no need of his daughters and knew perfectly well what they wanted.\n\nWhat follows in the Quran's account is not another attempt at persuasion. The next verse steps back from the scene entirely and delivers a judgement on the state these men had reached, in the form of an oath.",
  "72": "\"By your life ˹O Prophet˺, they certainly wandered blindly, intoxicated ˹by lust˺.\" is an extraordinary verse, and its opening phrase is unique in the Quran.\n\nAllah swears by the life of the Prophet Muhammad. Commentators have noted that this is the only place in the Book where such an oath appears, and Ibn Abbas is reported to have said that Allah did not create a soul more honoured to Him than Muhammad, and that he never heard Allah swear by the life of anyone else.\n\nThe honour in that is considerable, and it is placed in the middle of an account about Lot's people, addressed to a Prophet who was at that moment being called insane by his own community. While the Meccans mocked him in the street, this verse was recited among them.\n\nIt is worth adding that Allah may swear by whatever He wills of His creation, while human beings are permitted to swear only by Allah. The Prophet stated this clearly in a hadith recorded by al-Bukhari and Muslim.\n\nWhat the oath affirms is a description of the men outside Lot's door. The Arabic indicates them wandering about in a state of confusion, and the second term indicates intoxication.\n\nThe two words together describe a condition rather than a decision. These were people whose desire had reached the point where judgement no longer operated. They had been offered a lawful alternative moments earlier and could not see it, they had been warned for years and could not hear it, and they were about to be destroyed within hours.\n\nThat is what the intoxication describes. Not drunkenness from a substance, but appetite that had grown until it displaced everything else, including self-preservation.\n\nThere is a warning in the image that applies well beyond this particular sin. Any desire allowed to grow unchecked eventually consumes the faculty that would have restrained it.",
  "73": "\"So the ˹mighty˺ blast overtook them at sunrise.\" delivers the outcome, and it arrives exactly when the angels had said it would.\n\nThe Arabic word indicates a single overwhelming cry or sound. The same term was used in Surah Hud for the end of Thamud and of Midian, and commentators generally understand these descriptions as different aspects of one event rather than competing accounts.\n\nThe timing is the detail worth dwelling on. The blast came at sunrise, precisely as the decree revealed to Lot had specified, and Surah Hud recorded the angels asking him whether the morning was not near.\n\nSunrise is the hour when a city wakes. Ovens are lit, animals are moved, and the day's business begins. These men had been at Lot's door through the night, rejoicing, and the sun came up on the last morning they would see.\n\nThere is something in the Quran's consistent choice of ordinary hours for these destructions. Nothing is described as arriving during a battle or a crisis. Surah Yunus asked whether people had considered that His torment might come upon them by night or by day, and the answer given throughout these accounts is that it comes while life is proceeding normally.\n\nThe word for overtaking them indicates that it seized them, catching them where they were. There was no warning within the moment itself, no interval in which to run, and no defence available.\n\nWhat makes the timing particularly stark is what had happened only hours earlier. Lot had pleaded with them at his door, offered a lawful alternative, and asked them to fear Allah. Every one of those appeals was answered with mockery, and the interval between the mockery and the outcome was a single night.\n\nThe following verse describes what accompanied the blast, and it adds a second element that made the destruction complete.",
  "74": "\"And We turned the cities ˹of Sodom and Gomorrah˺ upside down\" describes the second element of the destruction, and the image is total.\n\nThe Arabic indicates making the highest part the lowest, overturning the settlements entirely. The Quran refers to these places elsewhere as the overturned towns, and Surah an-Najm describes the overturned city being hurled down.\n\nCommentators have noted a correspondence in this that is difficult to miss, though it should be offered as reflection rather than as established interpretation. A society that had inverted the natural order, made hospitality an offence, and forbidden a man from protecting the vulnerable was ended by being physically inverted.\n\n\"And rained upon them stones of baked clay.\" adds what followed the overturning.\n\nThe Arabic indicates stones of hardened clay, and Surah Hud describes them as clustered and marked, coming in succession rather than singly. That chapter added that each was designated, which conveys precision rather than randomness.\n\nThe two elements together removed every direction of escape. The ground itself turned over, and the sky delivered what followed. A person who survived one would meet the other.\n\nThe region associated with these cities lies near the Dead Sea, and the landscape there still bears signs of ancient upheaval. The Quran does not identify a precise site, and there is no need to insist on one.\n\nWhat the Quran does insist on is that the ruins were visible and known, which the following verses state directly.\n\nFor the Meccan audience, this account was not distant history. Their caravans travelled north through that region twice a year, and Surah Hud closed its version of this episode by telling them that such stones are not far from the wrongdoers, a line that could be read geographically and morally at once.\n\nThe stones themselves answer the city's prosperity. The plain was known for water and greenery, which is part of why its people could not imagine ruin reaching them there. What a fertile setting gives, its Maker can replace with something else entirely, and the Quran leaves the site exposed on a public road rather than hidden.",
  "75": "\"Surely in this are signs for those who contemplate.\" states who benefits from the account just given, and the Arabic term used for them is worth examining.\n\nThe word indicates those who examine closely, who read indications and draw conclusions from them. Its root relates to the mark left by something, so the person described is one who notices traces and works out what produced them.\n\nCommentators have explained it as those with insight, those who reflect on evidence and understand what it points toward. Some connected it to physical observation, the ability to look at remains and read the history in them.\n\nThat fits the context exactly. The ruins of these cities lay along a route that people travelled, and the following verse says so directly. Everyone who passed saw the same stones. What distinguished one traveller from another was whether he read them.\n\nThere is a distinction being drawn here that runs through the Quran. Evidence is public and available; the capacity to receive it is not evenly distributed, and the difference has to do with attention and honesty rather than intelligence.\n\nSurah Yusuf made the same observation in different words, noting how many signs in the heavens and the earth people pass by with indifference.\n\nThe plural form, signs rather than a sign, is also worth noticing. The account contains several. There is the destruction itself, and the deliverance of Lot's household. There is the fate of a wife who lived in a prophet's house and was not saved. There is the speed with which a society moved from mockery at a doorway to elimination at sunrise. And there is the appointed timing, announced in advance and kept precisely.\n\nEach of these is a separate indication, and a person reading the account carefully can extract them all.\n\nThe verse that follows makes the evidence concrete by pointing at something the Prophet's audience could see for themselves.",
  "76": "\"Their ruins still lie along a known route.\" brings the account out of history and places it on a road the Prophet's audience travelled.\n\nThe Arabic indicates that these remains are on a way that is established, a road still in use. Commentators identified it as the route running north from Arabia toward Syria, which passed through the region associated with these cities.\n\nThe Quraysh were merchants. Their winter and summer caravans are mentioned in Surah Quraysh, and the northern journey took them past exactly the area described here.\n\nSo the verse is not offering an argument from ancient records. It is pointing out a window. The evidence for what happened to Lot's people was something a Meccan trader could observe on his next commercial trip, and Surah as-Saffat states plainly that they pass by them in the morning and at night, asking whether they will not use reason.\n\nThere is something worth noticing about the Quran's consistent method here. It repeatedly directs people toward physical, verifiable things: ruins on trade routes, the alternation of day and night, rain producing crops, the growth of a child. Very little is asked to be accepted purely on report.\n\nThe phrase about a known route also carries a quieter implication. These ruins were not hidden in some inaccessible place requiring expedition and expertise. They were beside a road everybody used.\n\nThat is often how evidence works. What people fail to notice is usually not concealed but familiar, passed daily until it stops registering.\n\nThe Prophet later instructed his companions, when they camped near the ruins of Thamud on the way to Tabuk, not to enter the dwellings of those who wronged themselves except while weeping, in a hadith recorded by al-Bukhari and Muslim. Ruins are to be read rather than toured, and the verse that follows names who is capable of reading them.",
  "77": "\"Surely in this is a sign for those who believe.\" closes the account of Lot's people with a second statement about who benefits, and the group named here is different from the one named two verses earlier.\n\nThe first statement identified those who examine closely and read indications. This one identifies believers.\n\nThe pairing is not accidental, and it describes two stages of the same process. Careful observation is what allows a person to notice what the ruins mean. Belief is what allows him to act on it.\n\nThat distinction is worth drawing out, because the two are frequently separated. A historian can study a destroyed settlement thoroughly, establish exactly what happened, and take nothing personal from it. The information enters as data rather than as a warning.\n\nSomeone who believes reads the same evidence as a message directed at him. The difference is not in the quality of the observation but in whether the observer considers himself included in the account.\n\nThere is also something worth noticing about the singular here. The earlier verse mentioned signs in the plural, describing the many indications a careful examiner can extract. This verse mentions a sign, singular, which some commentators understood as the whole episode functioning as one unified proof for anyone who believes.\n\nThe chapter has used this closing formula repeatedly, and it will use it again in the verses ahead when it turns to the people of the Forest and to the dwellers of the rocky valley. Each account ends by naming the audience who will benefit, and the repetition builds a cumulative point.\n\nEvidence is not the scarce commodity. The Quran keeps supplying it, pointing at ruins, roads, skies, and rainfall, and then noting each time that its usefulness depends on the state of the person receiving it.\n\nThe next verse turns to another community, and the pattern begins again with a different people in a different place.",
  "78": "\"And the residents of the Forest were truly wrongdoers,\" introduces the next community, and the description is compressed to a single charge.\n\nThe Arabic word indicates a dense grove of trees, and commentators generally identified these people with the community of Midian, to whom the prophet Shu'ayb was sent. Surah ash-Shu'ara mentions the residents of the Forest and names Shu'ayb as their messenger, which supports the identification.\n\nSome scholars discussed whether these were the same people as Midian or a neighbouring community sharing the same prophet. The difference is a matter of historical detail rather than meaning, and the Quran's purpose in mentioning them is the same either way.\n\nTheir location fits the pattern the chapter has been building. Midian lay in the north-west of the Arabian peninsula, along the same trade corridor as the ruins mentioned in the preceding verses. Another destroyed community, another site on a route people travelled.\n\nThe charge against them is stated without elaboration: they were wrongdoers. The Arabic word indicates those who put things where they do not belong, and it covers injustice in every form.\n\nSurah Hud described their specific offences at length. They were a trading people who cheated in weights and measures, gave short measure, defrauded customers, and treated their commercial freedom as beyond religious regulation. When Shu'ayb corrected them, they asked sarcastically whether his prayers required them to abandon their forefathers' religion or to stop doing as they pleased with their own wealth.\n\nThe single word used here gathers all of that together. Commercial fraud is not treated as a lesser category of wrongdoing; it belongs under the same heading as the offences of the communities named before them in this chapter.\n\nThat grouping is itself instructive. A reader might expect the people of the Forest to be treated as a milder case than those just described. The Quran places them in the same sequence, and the following verse gives them the same ending.",
  "79": "\"So We inflicted punishment upon them.\" delivers the outcome for the residents of the Forest, and it is stated in a single clause without any description.\n\nThe contrast with the previous account is worth noticing. Lot's people received several verses describing what happened: the blast at sunrise, the overturning of the cities, and the stones. Here nothing is described at all.\n\nOther chapters supply the detail. Surah ash-Shu'ara mentions the punishment of a day of overshadowing gloom, connected with a cloud they had themselves demanded. Surah Hud describes a blast that left them fallen lifeless in their homes, using the same terms it used for Thamud.\n\nThe brevity here is deliberate. The chapter is building a sequence, and by this point the reader knows the shape of it. A community receives a messenger, refuses him, persists, and ends.\n\n\"The ruins of both nations still lie on a well-known road.\" repeats the point made a few verses earlier about Lot's people, and it now links two destroyed communities to the same route.\n\nBoth sites lay along the trade corridor running north from Arabia. The Meccans passed the region associated with Lot's cities and the territory of Midian on the same journey.\n\nThe Arabic indicates a clear, evident road, one that is plainly marked and regularly used.\n\nThere is a cumulative effect in this repetition that the chapter is working toward. One set of ruins might be dismissed as an isolated disaster. Two sets, on the same road, following the same pattern, with the same cause, is harder to explain away.\n\nThe Prophet's audience was being asked to consider a landscape rather than a story. Whatever they thought of him personally, the evidence he was pointing at was not something he had produced or could have arranged.\n\nThe chapter now turns to a third community, and this one was even closer to home.",
  "80": "\"Indeed, the residents of the Stone Valley also denied the messengers.\" introduces the third destroyed community in this sequence, and this one was the closest of all to the Prophet's audience.\n\nThe Arabic name indicates a place of rock, and it refers to the settlement of Thamud in the north-west of Arabia. Their carved dwellings still stand at the site known today as Mada'in Salih, and the Prophet's companions passed them during the expedition to Tabuk.\n\nThe prophet sent to them was Salih, whose account is given at length in Surah Hud. He called them to worship Allah alone, they demanded a sign, they were given the she-camel, and they killed it after being warned to leave it alone. Three days later they were destroyed.\n\nWhat is worth noticing here is the plural: they denied the messengers. Only Salih is mentioned as sent to Thamud, and the same plural appeared in Surah Hud regarding Ad.\n\nCommentators have explained this in complementary ways. Rejecting one messenger amounts to rejecting the message all of them brought, since the essential call was identical in every case. Someone who refuses the principle refuses everyone who ever delivered it.\n\nThat explanation has a practical dimension. A person who dismisses a message because of something about the messenger has not actually engaged with the message, and would dismiss the next one who brought it for a different reason.\n\nThe geographical detail matters for the chapter's argument. The ruins of Lot's people and of Midian lay to the north, on the Syrian route. Thamud's remains lay on the same road, closer to Arabia, and their carved facades are unusually well preserved.\n\nThe Meccans therefore had three sets of evidence available on a single trade journey, and the Quran had already recounted what happened at each.\n\nThe following verses describe how Thamud received their signs, and what they trusted in instead.",
  "81": "\"We gave them Our signs, but they turned away from them.\" states what Thamud received and what they did with it.\n\nThe signs were considerable. Salih's people had demanded proof, and they were given the she-camel described in Surah Hud, which was designated as a sign for them with a share of the water on alternating days. They were warned to leave her alone and told that harming her would bring a swift punishment.\n\nBeyond the camel, they had everything the chapter has already described: an ordered sky, a spread earth, measured provision, and a messenger from among their own people.\n\n\"But they turned away from them\" describes the response with a verb indicating deliberate avoidance, turning one's back and moving away.\n\nThat is a specific kind of refusal, and it is worth distinguishing from others. They did not examine the evidence and find it insufficient. They did not argue that the camel was a trick, as Pharaoh's court argued about Moses. They turned away, which is what people do with something they would rather not deal with.\n\nThere is a familiar quality to this. Most evasion of uncomfortable truth is not intellectual. It is a change of subject, a decision not to look, a busyness that leaves no room for the question.\n\nThe Quran describes the same behaviour elsewhere in vivid terms, mentioning people who put their fingers in their ears and cover themselves with their garments to avoid hearing.\n\nWhat makes Thamud's case particularly stark is that they had asked for the sign. Having demanded evidence and received exactly what they asked for, they walked away from it, and then killed it.\n\nThat sequence exposes what such demands usually are. A person genuinely seeking proof changes his position when he receives it, and someone who does not change his position was never seeking proof.\n\nThe next verse describes what they relied on instead of what they had been given.",
  "82": "\"They carved their homes in the mountains, feeling secure.\" describes what Thamud trusted in, and the detail is the key to understanding their fate.\n\nThey were skilled stonemasons. The Arabic indicates cutting and hewing dwellings out of the rock face, and the remains of that work still stand in the north-west of Arabia, with elaborate carved facades cut directly into cliffs.\n\nSurah al-Fajr mentions them hewing out the rocks in the valley, and Surah ash-Shu'ara records Salih asking whether they thought they would be left secure among gardens, springs, crops, and houses carved with skill.\n\nThe word for their security is the point of the verse. They felt safe.\n\nTheir confidence was not irrational. Stone dwellings resist weather, fire, raiders, and time. Anyone standing in front of those facades would understand why the people who made them believed they had solved the problem of vulnerability.\n\nWhat the verse identifies is the gap between engineering and actual security. Their walls were genuinely strong, and the thing that came for them was not the kind of threat walls address.\n\nThere is a wider observation here that applies to every generation. People build defences against the dangers they can imagine, and feel secure in proportion to the quality of those defences. Insurance, savings, medicine, and locked doors all address real risks, and none of them addresses the appointed term this chapter described at its opening.\n\nThe Quran repeatedly connects a sense of security with the moment of destruction. Surah al-A'raf asks whether the people of the towns felt secure against Allah's plan coming to them at night while they slept, or in the morning while they played.\n\nThe chapter supplied the correction in advance. Its opening verses stated that no community is destroyed without a destined term, and that none can advance or postpone it. Carved facades change none of that; the appointment keeps its schedule regardless of the stone it finds standing in the way.\n\nThe following verse states what happened, and its timing makes the point about their carved fortifications with a brevity that requires no commentary.",
  "83": "\"But the ˹mighty˺ blast overtook them in the morning,\" delivers the outcome, and the instrument chosen exposes the emptiness of everything the previous verse described.\n\nThe Arabic word indicates a single overwhelming cry or sound. Other passages describe Thamud's end with related terms, including a thunderbolt and a violent shaking, and commentators generally understand these as aspects of one event.\n\nA sound is what came for them.\n\nConsider what that means for a people who had carved their homes into solid rock. Stone resists almost everything: wind, rain, fire, siege, and centuries. It offers no resistance whatsoever to a sound. Whatever they had built, the thing that arrived passed through it as though it were not there.\n\nThere is a pattern in these accounts that becomes visible when they are read together. Noah's people were drowned in a place where they expected no water. Ad, who boasted of their physical strength, were destroyed by moving air. Lot's cities, built on solid ground, were turned over. Thamud, secure inside carved stone, were ended by a noise.\n\nIn each case the thing they relied on proved irrelevant, and in several cases the instrument was the opposite of what their defences addressed.\n\nThe timing is the second detail. Morning again, as with Lot's people, which the chapter mentioned only a few verses earlier.\n\nThat repetition is not coincidental. The Quran consistently describes these destructions arriving at ordinary hours, when a settlement wakes and the day begins. Nothing is described as happening during a crisis or a battle.\n\nSurah Hud recorded that Salih had told them plainly they had three days to enjoy life in their homes, and called it a promise that would not fail.\n\nThey spent those three days inside the strongest houses in Arabia, and the morning arrived exactly on schedule.",
  "84": "\"And all they achieved was of no help to them.\" closes the account of Thamud with an assessment of everything they had built.\n\nThe Arabic indicates that what they used to earn, acquire, and accomplish did not avail them in the slightest.\n\nTheir achievements were real. The carved dwellings still stand, which is more than can be said for most ancient construction, and visitors to the site can see the quality of the workmanship. These were capable people whose skill outlasted them by thousands of years.\n\nThe verse does not deny any of that. It says the achievements provided no help at the one moment when help was required.\n\nThere is a distinction being drawn that runs through the whole Quran. Things can be genuinely valuable within their proper domain and completely useless outside it. A carved house is an excellent house. It is not a defence against a decree.\n\nThe same word for availing appeared in Surah Yunus regarding assumptions that cannot take the place of truth, and in Surah Hud regarding the gods that were of no help when the command of the Lord came.\n\nWhat makes this verse particularly pointed is the word for what they earned. It implies effort, accumulation, and the satisfaction of having produced something. A lifetime of work is being described, and then dismissed in a single clause.\n\nThe relevance to the chapter's audience is direct. The Meccans were traders whose wealth and standing were the measure of their confidence, and this chapter opened by describing people absorbed in eating, enjoying themselves, and being diverted by long hopes.\n\nThamud's ruins were on their trade route, and the argument the chapter is making does not require any elaboration beyond pointing at them.\n\nThree communities have now been described in sequence, all destroyed, all on roads the audience travelled. The chapter now turns from history to the purpose behind the whole arrangement.",
  "85": "\"We have not created the heavens and the earth and everything in between except for a purpose.\" turns from three destroyed communities to the reason any of it matters.\n\nThe Arabic phrase indicates creation with truth, meaning for a genuine end rather than as play or accident. The Quran presses this claim repeatedly, asking elsewhere whether people thought they were created purposelessly and would not be returned.\n\nPlacing it here draws the accounts together. The destructions just described were not random catastrophes in a meaningless universe. They occurred within an arrangement that has a purpose, and the purpose is what makes accountability possible.\n\n\"And the Hour is certain to come\" states the consequence. If creation has a purpose, it has an end at which the purpose is fulfilled.\n\n\"So forgive graciously.\" is the instruction that follows, and its placement is genuinely surprising.\n\nAfter three accounts of communities destroyed for rejecting their messengers, addressed to a Prophet whose own people were mocking him and calling him insane, the instruction given is to forgive.\n\nThe Arabic phrase indicates a beautiful overlooking, pardoning without reproach or bitterness. Commentators explained it as forgiving in a manner that involves no rebuke afterwards, letting something go completely rather than holding it in reserve.\n\nThe logic connecting the two halves of the verse is worth drawing out. Because the Hour is certain, and because judgement belongs to Allah, the Prophet is released from the need to settle accounts himself. Certainty about the outcome is precisely what makes gracious pardon possible.\n\nA person who believes nothing will ever be put right must seek redress personally. Someone certain that everything will be addressed can afford to let insults pass.\n\nThis same disposition appeared throughout the account of Joseph, who forgave brothers who had destroyed his childhood and prayed for their forgiveness.",
  "86": "\"Surely your Lord is the Master Creator, All-Knowing.\" gives the two names that ground everything stated in the previous verse.\n\nThe first indicates the One who creates continuously and completely, the Creator in the fullest sense. Its form in Arabic conveys constancy: not one who created once and stopped, but one whose creating is ongoing.\n\nThat name answers the first half of the previous verse, which stated that the heavens and the earth were created for a purpose. A creator who fashioned something and abandoned it might leave a universe with no follow-through. This name describes the opposite.\n\nThe second name indicates complete knowledge. It answers the second half of the previous verse, concerning the certainty of the Hour, since a reckoning is only meaningful if everything is known.\n\nTogether the two names cover the whole arrangement. He made it, and He knows it, which means the purpose will be fulfilled and the accounting will be accurate.\n\nThere is a further connection worth noticing. The verse before it instructed the Prophet to forgive graciously, and this verse tells him who his Lord is.\n\nForgiving requires confidence in someone else handling the matter. A person who overlooks an injury is not saying the injury did not occur; he is declining to pursue it himself. That is only reasonable if someone with complete knowledge is keeping the record.\n\nThe word \"your Lord\" is worth noticing too. The Arabic \"Rabb\" indicates the One who owns, sustains, nurtures, and cares for. In a chapter addressed to a Prophet under sustained mockery, the reminder is personal rather than abstract.\n\nHe is being told that the One who made everything and knows everything is his own Lord, and that this relationship is the basis on which he can afford to let insults pass without carrying them.\n\nThe following verse names a specific gift given to him, which functions as evidence of exactly this care.",
  "87": "\"We have certainly granted you the seven often-repeated verses and the great Quran.\" names two gifts, and the first has been the subject of considerable discussion.\n\nThe Arabic phrase indicates seven of the repeated ones. The most widely accepted explanation, supported by an authentic hadith recorded by al-Bukhari, is that this refers to Surah al-Fatihah, the opening chapter of seven verses. The Prophet told a companion that al-Fatihah is the seven oft-repeated verses and the great Quran that he was given.\n\nSome early commentators offered other views, including that it refers to seven long chapters of the Quran. The Prophet's own explanation settles the matter for most scholars.\n\nThe description of these verses as often-repeated is accurate in a way that has only become more obvious with time. Al-Fatihah is recited in every unit of every prayer, which means a Muslim performing the five daily prayers recites it at least seventeen times a day, and it has been recited that way by millions of people for fourteen centuries.\n\nThe second gift is the Quran itself, described as great.\n\nThe placement of this verse is what gives it force. The Prophet was being mocked, his people were demanding angels, and he was watching wealthy opponents enjoy comfort he did not share. This verse tells him what he actually has.\n\nThe next verse makes the comparison explicit by instructing him not to look longingly at what others have been given.\n\nThe granting is also communal. The Prophet received these verses, and through him every Muslim received them; no other passage of the Quran is placed on the tongue of every worshipper in every prayer, whatever a person's learning, age, or native tongue. A gift given to one man turned out to be the inheritance of everyone who followed him.\n\nThere is something in this arrangement that applies well beyond the original situation. A person who feels the lack of what others possess is often carrying something considerably more valuable without registering it, and the remedy the Quran applies here is not to argue about the value of wealth but to name what he already holds.",
  "88": "\"Do not let your eyes crave the ˹fleeting˺ pleasures We have provided for some of the disbelievers\" addresses a temptation that follows naturally from the previous verse.\n\nThe Prophet had just been reminded of what he was given: the seven repeated verses and the great Quran. Now he is told not to stretch his eyes toward what others were given instead.\n\nThe Arabic indicates extending the gaze, looking with longing at something one does not have. The image is precise. It describes the eye lingering rather than passing over.\n\nWhat the disbelievers had been given was material comfort. The Meccan leaders were wealthy, and the small Muslim community was largely poor, with many of its members enslaved or without protection.\n\nNote that the verse does not deny they had been given something real. It says We have provided it, which is an honest acknowledgement.\n\nWhat it disputes is the value of the comparison. The Quran elsewhere describes such provision as a temporary enjoyment, and Surah Ta-Ha uses almost identical language, instructing the Prophet not to extend his eyes toward what has been given as the splendour of worldly life, adding that the provision of his Lord is better and more lasting.\n\n\"Nor grieve for them.\" addresses a different reaction, and its inclusion is worth noticing.\n\nThe Prophet's distress was not envy. Commentators have generally understood this grief as sorrow over their refusal to believe, which several verses in the Quran describe him experiencing intensely.\n\n\"And be gracious to the believers.\" gives the positive instruction, and the Arabic image is lowering one's wing, the way a bird lowers its wing over its young. It indicates gentleness, protection, and humility toward those under one's care.\n\nThe three instructions together redirect attention completely: away from what others have, away from grief over their choices, and toward the people who are actually present.",
  "89": "\"\"And say, \"I am truly sent with a clear warning\"—\" is the declaration the Prophet is instructed to make, and it follows a series of instructions about where his attention should rest.\n\nHaving been told not to look longingly at what others possess, not to grieve over their refusal, and to be gentle with the believers, he is now told what to announce about himself.\n\nThe statement is short and contains no argument. He is not told to debate, defend himself against the accusation of insanity recorded earlier in this chapter, or produce the angels they had demanded. He states his role.\n\nThe Arabic indicates a warner who makes things clear, and commentators have noted that the description covers both the clarity of the warning and the clarity of the one delivering it. There is nothing ambiguous about either.\n\nThat word for clarity has appeared throughout this chapter. It opened by describing the Book as the clear Quran, and the flare pursuing eavesdroppers was described as clearly visible.\n\nBeing a warner is a role the Quran assigns to the Prophet repeatedly, and it carries a specific limit that the surrounding chapters have made explicit. A warner delivers; he does not compel, guarantee results, or manufacture belief.\n\nThere is relief in that limitation for anyone carrying a message. The Prophet's success was never measured by how many accepted, and Surah ar-Ra'd stated plainly that his duty was only to deliver while the reckoning belongs to Allah.\n\nThe Prophet described his own position with an image recorded in al-Bukhari and Muslim, comparing himself to a man who has seen an approaching army and runs to warn his people, calling out to them because he saw what they could not.\n\nThat image captures why the warning is delivered with urgency rather than detachment, and why the warner keeps calling even when the people he is calling are laughing at him.",
  "90": "\"˹A warning˺ similar to what We sent to those who divided ˹the Scriptures˺,\" connects the Prophet's warning to something delivered before, and the group named requires explanation.\n\nThe Arabic word indicates those who divided or apportioned something. Commentators have offered several explanations, and they are worth presenting honestly.\n\nMany understood it as referring to earlier communities of the People of the Book who divided their scripture, accepting some of it and rejecting the rest, which fits the following verse exactly.\n\nOthers explained it as a group of Meccans who divided the roads into the city during the pilgrimage season, stationing themselves on different routes to intercept arriving travellers and warn them against the Prophet. Reports of this activity exist in the historical literature, and it was an organised campaign.\n\nBoth readings describe a form of dividing, and both fit the context. The first concerns dividing a message; the second concerns dividing access to one.\n\nWhat the verse establishes is that the Prophet's warning belongs to a series. The same warning was sent before, and the people who received it dealt with it in a particular way.\n\nThat connection serves the chapter's purpose. The Prophet was facing something with precedent, and knowing this changes how the difficulty is experienced.\n\nThere is also an implicit caution for the Muslim community in this verse. If earlier communities were warned about dividing their scripture, the warning is available to anyone holding a scripture, and the Quran repeatedly notes that earlier peoples divided only after clear knowledge came to them.\n\nThe two readings converge in any case. Whether a community splits its scripture into accepted and rejected portions, or splits the roads so that travellers hear only one side, the method is identical: control what people encounter, and you shape what they believe they know. The warning stands against both, and the next verse shows the same habit at work on the revelation itself.\n\nThe following verse describes the dividing precisely, and the description turns out to be uncomfortably recognisable in every religious community, including those who consider themselves faithful to a text.",
  "91": "\"Who ˹now˺ accept parts of the Quran, rejecting others.\" describes what the dividing actually consisted of.\n\nThe Arabic indicates making something into portions or fragments. Commentators have explained the reference in more than one way, and the range is worth noting. Many understood it as accepting some of the revelation and refusing the rest. Others explained it as fragmenting the Book by calling it poetry, magic, or ancient tales, dividing it up with various dismissive labels.\n\nBoth descriptions identify the same underlying behaviour: treating a message as something to be sorted rather than received.\n\nSelective acceptance is a recognisable habit, and the chapter is right to name it. A person encounters a body of guidance and finds that some of it suits him. Those parts are embraced enthusiastically, quoted, and defended. The parts that conflict with his interests, culture, or desires are quietly set aside, explained away, or treated as less important.\n\nWhat makes this so difficult to detect is that the person doing it genuinely believes in the source. He is not rejecting the Book; he is arranging it.\n\nThe Quran addresses this elsewhere with unusual severity. Surah al-Baqarah asks a community whether they believe in part of the Scripture and disbelieve in another part, and states that the recompense for such conduct is disgrace in worldly life and severe punishment on the Day of Resurrection.\n\nThe logic behind that severity is straightforward. If a person accepts only what he already agrees with, the source has no authority over him. He is following his own judgement and using revelation to decorate it.\n\nThis is not a warning aimed only at earlier communities. Every religious tradition, including Muslims, produces people who hold their scripture in high honour and read it selectively.\n\nThe verses that follow state what happens to such people, and the answer applies to everyone without distinction.",
  "92": "\"So by your Lord! We will certainly question them all\" is an oath followed by a promise, and the combination gives it considerable force.\n\nAllah swears by Himself, described here as the Prophet's Lord, and the Arabic construction that follows is emphatic in multiple ways: a particle of emphasis and an emphatic verb ending, both reinforcing the certainty of the questioning.\n\nOaths in the Quran are used when something needs to be settled beyond dispute, and what is being settled here is that everyone will be asked.\n\nThe phrase \"all of them\" is the part that carries the weight. Not the leaders only, not the notorious, not those who committed obvious crimes. Every one of them.\n\nThat scope is worth pausing over, because people naturally assume categories of exemption. The follower assumes his leader carries the responsibility, and Surah Ibrahim recorded exactly that assumption collapsing in the exchange between the weak and the arrogant. The obscure person assumes he is too insignificant to be examined.\n\nThe verse removes both assumptions with a single word.\n\nThe use of \"your Lord\" in the oath is also worth noticing. In a chapter addressed to a Prophet whose people were dividing his message, accepting parts and rejecting others, the oath is sworn by the One who is his Lord specifically.\n\nThere is reassurance in that framing as well as warning. The people who fragmented the revelation and mocked the one who brought it are going to be asked about it, and the questioning is guaranteed by an oath.\n\nWhat they will be questioned about is stated in the following verse, and the answer turns out to be broader than the specific offence just described. The examination is not limited to how they treated the Book, which means the guarantee applies to everyone reading it as much as to those it originally addressed.",
  "93": "\"About what they used to do.\" completes the oath begun in the previous verse, and it defines the subject of the questioning.\n\nThe Arabic indicates what they were doing habitually, using a construction that conveys ongoing, repeated action rather than isolated incidents. The examination concerns the pattern of a life rather than a list of dramatic moments.\n\nThat is worth dwelling on. People tend to imagine judgement as an examination of a few significant decisions, the crises and turning points where character was visibly tested. The wording here points somewhere else: at what a person was in the habit of doing.\n\nHabits are made of small things repeated. How a person spoke to those who could do nothing for him, whether he prayed when nobody expected it, what he did with an hour nobody was accounting for. None of these register as events, and together they constitute most of a life.\n\nThe scope of the questioning is also unrestricted. The previous verses described people dividing the revelation, accepting parts and rejecting others, and this verse does not limit the examination to that offence.\n\nThe Prophet described the shape of this questioning in a hadith recorded by at-Tirmidhi, saying that the feet of a servant will not move on the Day of Resurrection until he is asked about four things: his life and how he spent it, his knowledge and what he did with it, his wealth, where he earned it and how he spent it, and his body and how he used it.\n\nEvery item on that list concerns ordinary conduct rather than exceptional moments.\n\nThere is a practical use for this verse that costs nothing to apply. A person can examine what he actually does with his ordinary days, since that is precisely what the question will be about, and adjusting a habit is considerably easier than reversing a lifetime.",
  "94": "\"So proclaim what you have been commanded, and turn away from the polytheists.\" gives the Prophet a direct instruction, and commentators have identified this as a significant moment in the Meccan period.\n\nThe Arabic verb indicates striking through or splitting, and it conveys announcing something openly and forcefully rather than speaking quietly. Several early commentators reported that the Prophet had been calling to Islam privately for a period, and that this verse marked the beginning of open, public proclamation.\n\nWhether or not one accepts every detail of those reports, the instruction itself is clear. What he had been commanded was to be announced without concealment.\n\nThe content is specified precisely: what you have been commanded. Not his own views, not what would be well received, and not an adjusted version calculated to reduce hostility. The message as given.\n\nThat limitation protects a caller in both directions. He is not free to add, and he is not permitted to subtract.\n\n\"And turn away from the polytheists.\" is the second half, and it needs to be understood correctly.\n\nThe Arabic indicates disregarding them, paying no attention to their objections and mockery. It does not mean ceasing to convey the message to them, since the verse has just commanded open proclamation to everyone.\n\nWhat is being described is an attitude rather than a withdrawal. Announce the message publicly, and stop being affected by the reaction.\n\nThose two instructions belong together, and each is difficult without the other. Public proclamation invites public ridicule, and a person who remains sensitive to ridicule will soften what he says until nothing remains of it.\n\nThe chapter has already recorded what he was facing: being called insane, being challenged to produce angels, and watching his message fragmented into parts to be accepted or dismissed.\n\nThe assurance that makes this instruction possible follows immediately.",
  "95": "\"Surely We will be sufficient for you against the mockers,\" is the guarantee that accompanies the instruction to proclaim openly and disregard the opposition.\n\nThe Arabic indicates being enough for someone, taking care of a matter on his behalf so that he does not need to handle it himself.\n\nThe Prophet is not told to defend himself, answer the mockery, or organise protection. He is told that this particular problem has been taken over.\n\nThat is what makes the previous instruction possible. Announcing a message publicly in a hostile city, and then ignoring the reaction, requires knowing that someone else is dealing with the consequences.\n\nThe mockers are named specifically. This chapter opened with them calling him insane, and mockery has been the recurring weapon throughout: Noah laughed at while building, Hud accused of madness, Salih dismissed as a disappointment, and every messenger before him ridiculed, as this chapter stated plainly.\n\nHistorical reports mention a small group of Quraysh leaders who took the lead in this campaign, and several of them met unpleasant ends before the Prophet's migration. Commentators connect this verse with those events, and the details vary in strength across the reports.\n\nWhat is certain is the principle rather than any particular case. Allah undertook to handle the mockers, and the Prophet was freed from having to.\n\nThere is something in this worth extracting for anyone facing ridicule for their faith. The instinct is to answer, to prove the mockers wrong, to win the exchange. The verse offers a different arrangement: deliver the message, ignore the response, and leave that particular account to be settled elsewhere.\n\nThe guarantee also marks the limit of the Prophet's responsibility, and servants of the message still work inside that limit. Delivering was his task; outcomes were not. No messenger was ever charged with making people believe, and Allah's sufficiency against the mockers meant the account for mockery was never his to settle in the first place.\n\nThe following verse identifies who these mockers were in terms of their actual offence, which turns out to be considerably more serious than their treatment of one man.",
  "96": "\"Who set up ˹other˺ gods with Allah.\" identifies the mockers by their real offence rather than by their behaviour toward the Prophet.\n\nThe Arabic indicates making something a partner alongside Allah, treating another as a peer to Him.\n\nThat framing is significant. These people had been ridiculing a man in the streets of Mecca, and the verse describes their fundamental problem as something else entirely. Their mockery was a symptom.\n\nThere is a logic connecting the two. A person who assigns divine status to carved objects has already abandoned the standard by which claims are evaluated. Someone whose religion rests on inherited assumption rather than evidence will naturally treat a message based on evidence as absurd, since he has no category for it.\n\nSurah Yunus made this connection explicitly, describing them as following nothing but assumptions while assumptions can never take the place of truth.\n\n\"They will soon come to know.\" closes the verse with a phrase that carries considerable weight in its brevity.\n\nThe Arabic indicates knowledge that arrives through experience rather than through argument. They will not be convinced; they will find out.\n\nThe word for soon is doing quiet work here. From the standpoint of people who had been mocking for years without consequence, nothing seemed imminent. The chapter opened by describing exactly that attitude, telling them to eat, enjoy themselves, and be diverted by hope, because they would soon know.\n\nThat phrase now returns at the chapter's close, forming a frame around everything between: three destroyed communities, a guarded sky, the account of Iblis, and the instruction to proclaim openly.\n\nThe phrase 'with Allah' is the heart of the charge. The offence is not merely worshipping something other than Allah, which could be simple ignorance, but setting anything in His company, a claim about who deserves what belongs to Him alone. That is why the verse can name their crime without describing a single ritual: the arrangement itself, whoever the partners were, was the problem.\n\nFor the mockers, the promise was fulfilled within a few years. For the Prophet, it meant that the account he had been told to ignore was being kept accurately by someone else.",
  "97": "\"We certainly know that your heart is truly distressed by what they say.\" is an acknowledgement rather than an instruction, and its presence in the chapter is worth noticing.\n\nThe Arabic indicates a chest becoming constricted, tightening. It is a physical description of a familiar sensation: the tension that settles in the body under sustained pressure.\n\nThe Prophet is not told that he should not feel this. He is told that it is known.\n\nThat distinction matters a great deal. A person struggling under strain is often met with correction, told he should be stronger or should not let things affect him. The verse does none of that. It names the condition accurately and confirms that it has been seen.\n\nThere is a form of relief in being understood that no advice can provide, and the Quran offers it here before offering anything else.\n\nWhat was causing the distress is specified: what they say. Not physical danger, though there was plenty of that, but words. He was called insane, accused of fabrication, described as a poet and a soothsayer, and his message was fragmented into parts to be accepted or dismissed.\n\nWords wound in a way that is easy to underestimate, particularly for someone whose entire mission consisted of speech. Every insult aimed at his credibility was aimed at the thing he was carrying.\n\nSurah al-An'am offers a related consolation, telling him that it is not him they deny but the signs of Allah that the wrongdoers reject.\n\nThe knowledge named here cuts both ways in the kindest manner. Allah knew what was said and also what the saying did to the man who heard it, so no insult went unrecorded from either side. Taken with the promise of sufficiency in the previous verse, the arrangement is complete: the harm was fully seen, and the settling of it had been assigned.\n\nThe instruction that follows this acknowledgement is not to suppress the feeling or reason it away. What is prescribed in the next verse is something to do, and the remedy offered turns out to be worship rather than argument or self-discipline.",
  "98": "\"So glorify the praises of your Lord and be one of those who ˹always˺ pray,\" is the remedy prescribed for the distress just acknowledged, and it is worth examining why this remedy rather than another.\n\nThe Prophet was not told to argue better, avoid his critics, or reason himself out of the feeling. He was told to worship.\n\nThe Arabic phrase indicates declaring Allah free of every fault while praising Him. The two acts are joined: denying any deficiency and affirming every perfection. Muslims perform this constantly in the short formulas of remembrance recited after prayer and throughout the day.\n\nThere is a connection between this practice and the specific distress described. He was being told, day after day, that his message was false and that the One he worshipped had no exclusive claim. Glorification is the direct answer to that, and repeating it restores in the heart what the mockery attacks.\n\nBeing among those who prostrate is the second half, and it moves from words to posture.\n\nProstration is the position in which a person places his face on the ground, and the Prophet described it as the closest a servant comes to his Lord, in a hadith recorded by Muslim, encouraging abundant supplication in that state.\n\nFor a man whose dignity was under public assault, the prescribed response was to put his forehead on the floor before Allah. There is something in that which cuts through the entire logic of humiliation. A person whose standing rests on Allah cannot be diminished by people, and prostration is the physical enactment of that fact.\n\nIt is also worth noticing what prayer does practically. Distress isolates a person inside his own thoughts, and turning to worship interrupts the loop and directs attention outward and upward.\n\nThe Prophet's own practice reflected this. It is reported in the hadith literature that when a matter troubled him, he would hasten to prayer.",
  "99": "\"And worship your Lord until the inevitable comes your way.\" closes the chapter with a single instruction and a fixed endpoint.\n\nThe Arabic word rendered here as the inevitable indicates certainty, the thing about which there is no doubt. Commentators are agreed that it refers to death, and Ibn Abbas and others explained it in exactly those terms.\n\nDeath is described as the certainty because it is the one event nobody disputes. People argue about resurrection, about revelation, about the existence of a reckoning. Nobody argues about dying.\n\nWhat the verse establishes is the duration of worship: until that moment and not before. There is no stage of life at which a person graduates from it, no level of piety that permits retirement, and no achievement that closes the account early.\n\nThis has been misunderstood in a particular way that deserves correction. Some groups in Islamic history claimed that a person who reaches a certain spiritual state is relieved of religious obligations, reading the word for certainty as a station of knowledge rather than as death. Scholars rejected this firmly, and the reason is straightforward. The Prophet was the most certain of all human beings and worshipped until his final illness, praying while unable to stand.\n\nThere is also encouragement in the verse alongside the obligation. Worship continues until death, which means the door remains open until then. A person who has wasted decades still has the remainder, and Islamic teaching consistently affirms that repentance is accepted while life continues.\n\nThe chapter has arrived at a fitting close. It opened with people who would wish they had submitted, described their diversion by eating, enjoyment, and long hopes, and told them they would soon know.\n\nIt ends by telling the Prophet, and everyone reading, what to do with the interval: worship, until the certainty arrives."
};
